{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c95dc2db4d2aa02", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAllocation Before Ranking: Decoupled Token\n2\tCompression for OmniLLMs\n3\t\n4\tZhenghui Guo1 Yilin Yang1 Yuanbin Man2 Miao Yin2\n5\tWeidong Shi1 Rabimba Karanjai1 Omprakash Gnawali1 Chengming Zhang1\n6\t1Department of Computer Science, University of Houston\n7\t2Department of Computer Science and Engineering, The University of Texas at Arlington\n8\t\n9\tAbstract\n10\t\n11\tToken compression in OmniLLMs is typically posed as a single saliency-ranking\n12\tproblem: score each multimodal token, keep the top-K. We argue this abstraction\n13\tis mis-specified. The same attention score simultaneously decides two things: how\n14\tmuch retained capacity each modality receives, and which tokens within a modality\n15\tare kept. A shared top-K rule therefore inherits this audio-favoring allocation\n16\tprior, spending retained capacity on audio before video tokens have a chance\n17\tto compete. We propose MACER, a training-free compressor that first assigns\n18\texplicit audio and video budgets, then performs allocation-normalized ranking\n19\twithin each modality at modality-specific shallow layers. MACER significantly\n20\treduces token cost while preserving accuracy across audio-grounded, audio–video\n21\tjoint, visual-dominant, and video-centric benchmarks. At 25% retention, MACER\n22\tpreserves 98.7% of full-token performance on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 97.3% on\n23\tQwen2.5-Omni-3B. On Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, this 25% setting reaches OmniZip-\n24\tlevel performance at 45% retention while using lower FLOPs. On OmniVinci-9B,\n25\tthe same allocation-before-ranking principle improves over shared top-K ranking\n26\tby up to 12.9 points.\n27\t\n28\t1\n29\t\n30\tIntroduction\n31\t\n32\tOmnimodal large language models (OmniLLMs) unify audio and video understanding by interleaving\n33\tboth modalities and processing them through one decoder [Xu et al., 2025, Ye et al., 2025a, Fu et al.,\n34\t2024, Ge et al., 2025]. This design enables a single model to jointly understand audio and video,\n35\tbut it also makes inference scale with the combined multimodal sequence. Token compression is\n36\ttherefore necessary. Most existing compressors treat this problem as one saliency ranking: assigning\n37\ta score to each multimodal token and keeping the top-scoring tokens under a fixed budget. We argue\n38\tthat this ranking-only abstraction is incomplete for OmniLLM token compression.\n39\t\n40\tThe failure is “one score, two decisions.” In a shared decoder, attention magnitude both allocates\n41\tprobability mass across modalities and orders tokens within each modality. A shared top-K rule\n42\ttherefore uses the same score to decide how much audio/video capacity survives and which audio/video\n43\ttokens survive. These are different decisions: cross-modal capacity allocation should be controlled\n44\tbefore within-modality token selection, not induced accidentally by raw shared-attention mass. In\n45\tOmniLLM, the coupling is concrete: shared attention exhibits a persistent, content-responsive audio\n46\tallocation prior, so ranking audio and video tokens in one space can spend retained capacity on audio\n47\tbefore visual evidence has a fair chance to compete.\n48\t\n49\tOur diagnostics give three observations that motivate the method. Observation 1: shared attention\n50\tis not a neutral cross-modal saliency space. Even under video-essential queries, audio receives\n51\thigher attention mass per token across layers; null-content and distance controls show that the effect\n52\tis content-responsive rather than a fixed positional sink. Observation 2: the audio-favoring bias\n53\t\n54\tPreprint.\n55\t\n56\tarXiv:2608.01665v1  [cs.AI]  3 Aug 2026\fFigure 1: Shared attention favors audio across depth. ca and cv denote the mean audio and\n57\tvideo keys. (a) The query q is closer to ca than to cv, so shared attention favors audio. (b) Under\n58\tboth video-essential (VE) and audio-essential (AE) queries, log(massa/massv) stays positive across\n59\tlayers. (c) At L3 under VE queries, pooled query positions satisfy cos(q, ca) > cos(q, cv). This\n60\tgeometry illustrates why raw shared attention is not a neutral token-saliency space for omnimodal\n61\tcompression.\n62\t\n63\tis a budgeting problem, not a video-selection problem. ToMe-style [Bolya et al., 2023] video\n64\tmerging and random video dropping produce similar audio-mass trajectories at matched video keep\n65\tratio, while changing the retained audio share directly changes accuracy. Observation 3: audio\n66\tand video become selection-readable at different shallow depths. Audio readout is stable early,\n67\twhereas video readout is more layer-sensitive and improves around later shallow layers.\n68\t\n69\tWe propose MACER, a training-free inference-time compressor that instantiates this allocation-\n70\tselection principle. MACER first assigns explicit audio and video budgets, preventing retained\n71\tcapacity from being implicitly determined by raw shared-attention mass. It then ranks tokens only\n72\twithin each modality using allocation-normalized attention scores. Because audio and video saliency\n73\tbecome readable at different shallow depths, MACER reads them at modality-specific layers before\n74\tpruning. A lightweight video-local temporal coverage term is applied only inside the assigned video\n75\tbudget to reduce temporal over-concentration.\n76\t\n77\tWe evaluated MACER on three shared-decoder OmniLLM backbones (Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, Qwen2.5-\n78\tOmni-3B, and OmniVinci-9B) across four benchmarks that span audio-grounded, audio-video joint,\n79\tvisual-dominant, and video-centric tasks. MACER achieves the strongest aggregate performance\n80\tamong compared token-pruning baselines at every evaluated retention ratio. At 25% retention,\n81\tMACER preserves 98.7% of full-token performance on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B (vs. 91.6% for OmniZip).\n82\tOn Qwen2.5-Omni-3B, it preserves 97.3% (vs. 95.5% for OmniZip). On OmniVinci-9B, MACER\n83\toutperforms shared top-K ranking by up to 12.9 points, showing that the allocation–selection\n84\tseparation transfers across models.\n85\t\n86\t• We identify a structural failure of global Top-K omnimodal compression: a single importance\n87\tscore determines both the audio-video token budget and the tokens retained within each modality.\n88\tNo saliency signal fixes this coupling unless the token budget is allocated explicitly before\n89\tselection.\n90\t\n91\t• We propose MACER, a training-free compressor that decides how many audio and video tokens to\n92\tkeep before deciding which tokens to keep. MACER first allocates audio/video keep-ratios, then\n93\tranks tokens within each modality using attention normalized over that modality, and reads audio\n94\tand video saliency at different shallow layers. A small temporal-coverage rule prevents retained\n95\tvideo tokens from clustering in time. The method requires no training, no model changes, and no\n96\tsecond forward pass.\n97\t\n98\t• Our results validate that MACER preserves 98.7% / 97.3% of full-token accuracy on Qwen2.5-\n99\tOmni-7B/3B at 25% retention, and improves over shared top-K ranking by up to 12.9 points on\n100\tOmniVinci-9B.\n101\t\n102\t2\n103\t\n104\t\fFigure 2: Capacity allocation, not within-video selection, controls cross-modal balance. (a) A\n105\tshared top-K rule uses one score for two decisions. (b) ToMe and random dropping differ by at most\n106\t0.4 pp at matched video keep ratio, whereas reducing the video keep ratio itself shifts audio mass\n107\tshare by 9.0 pp. (c) The retained audio share α affects accuracy, with a video-heavy split (α = 0.10)\n108\toutperforming an audio-heavy split (α = 0.70) by 3.1 points.\n109\t\n110\t2 Related work\n111\t\n112\tModality competition in multimodal LLMs. Prior work has shown that MLLMs do not use\n113\tmodalities uniformly: modality bias appears across vision, audio, time-series, and graph inputs [Wu\n114\tet al., 2025, Zheng et al., 2025], and audio-language or audio-visual analyses show that cross-modal\n115\tattention can underuse task-relevant modalities [Wang et al., 2025]. These works mainly diagnose\n116\trepresentation or attention behavior. We study the deployment-time consequence for compression.\n117\tWhen audio and video tokens share a decoder softmax under a fixed retained-token budget, imbalance\n118\tbecomes a capacity-allocation problem: the compressor must decide both which tokens are salient\n119\tand how much capacity each modality receives.\n120\t\n121\tToken compression for multimodal LLMs. Multimodal token-compression methods differ by\n122\twhere the retention decision is made and what signal drives it. Encoder-side methods reduce visual,\n123\taudio-visual, or omnimodal context before or near decoder integration using encoder features, cross-\n124\tmodal guidance, or temporal/event structure [Tao et al., 2025b, Ding et al., 2026, Li and Huang,\n125\t2026, Guo et al., 2026, Shao et al., 2025b]. LLM-side methods instead prune or merge tokens using\n126\tattention, redundancy, or sparsification signals inside the model [Bolya et al., 2023, Chen et al., 2024,\n127\tShang et al., 2025, Yang et al., 2025a, Zhang et al., 2025b, Ye et al., 2025b, Xing et al., 2025, Huang\n128\tet al., 2025, Tao et al., 2025a, Jiang et al., 2025, Shao et al., 2025a], though recent VLM studies\n129\tquestion whether text-to-visual attention alone is reliable for visual-token importance [Zhang et al.,\n130\t2025a]. These methods provide useful saliency signals, but the audio-video budget is usually fixed,\n131\timplicit, or induced by a shared score. A single shared ranking therefore leaves capacity allocation\n132\tcoupled to within-modality token selection.\n133\t\n134\tOmnimodal token compression. The closest omnimodal line compresses audio-video contexts\n135\tdirectly. OmniZip [Tao et al., 2025b] uses an encoder-side audio-guided scheme before decoder\n136\tintegration, while OmniSIFT, DASH, and FastAV explore modality-asymmetric pruning, audio-driven\n137\tchunking, or audio-visual pruning [Ding et al., 2026, Li and Huang, 2026, Jung et al., 2026]. MACER\n138\tdiffers along the capacity-allocation axis: it exposes the retained audio share as an explicit deployment-\n139\tlevel control variable, then performs allocation-normalized selection within each modality using\n140\tshallow decoder-side attention at modality-specific layers. MACER is complementary to saliency-\n141\tsignal design: it targets a different axis, making the retained audio-video capacity split explicit before\n142\tmodality-local token selection.\n143\t\n144\t3 Shared Ranking Couples Allocation and Selection\n145\t\n146\tShared-budget omnimodal compression is often treated as a token-ranking problem: assign a saliency\n147\tscore to each audio and video token, then retain the top-scoring tokens under a common budget. This\n148\tabstraction is incomplete for shared-decoder OmniLLMs. In a shared attention [Vaswani et al., 2017],\n149\tattention magnitude does not only score token-level relevance; it also determines how probability\n150\t\n151\t3\n152\t\n153\t\fFigure 3: Audio and video become readable at different depths. We sweep one modality’s probe\n154\tlayer while fixing the other to MACER’s default. (a) Video readout is more layer-sensitive than audio\n155\treadout in most settings. (b) Video readout performs best at L4-L5. (c) Audio readout remains\n156\trelatively stable across layers.\n157\t\n158\tmass is allocated across modalities. A shared ranking rule therefore contains a cross-modal allocation\n159\tterm even when the desired decision is within-modality token selection.\n160\t\n161\tOne attention score, two decisions. A query’s attention to a token does two distinct things at once:\n162\tit scores how relevant that token is within its modality, and it decides how much probability mass\n163\tgoes to that modality versus the other (Fig. 2a). An exact decomposition makes this concrete. Let\n164\teach key decompose as k(m)\n165\tis the per-clip modality\n166\tcentroid and ϵ(m)\n167\texact decomposition\n168\t\n169\tis the within-modality residual. The log attention mass on modality m admits the\n170\t\n171\ti = cm + ϵ(m)\n172\t\n173\t, where cm = 1\n174\t|m|\n175\t\n176\ti∈m k(m)\n177\t\n178\t(cid:80)\n179\t\n180\ti\n181\t\n182\ti\n183\t\n184\ti\n185\t\n186\tlog massm(q) = q⊤cm\n187\t(cid:124) (cid:123)(cid:122) (cid:125)\n188\t∆alloc,m\n189\t\n190\t+ log\n191\t\n192\t(cid:88)\n193\t\n194\t(cid:16)\n195\t\n196\texp\n197\t\n198\tq⊤ϵ(m)\n199\ti\n200\t\n201\ti∈m\n202\t\n203\t(cid:124)\n204\t\n205\t(cid:123)(cid:122)\n206\t∆intra,m\n207\t\n208\t(cid:17)\n209\t\n210\t(cid:125)\n211\t\n212\t− log Z,\n213\t\n214\t(1)\n215\t\n216\twhere Z = (cid:80)\n217\tson, Z cancels:\n218\t\n219\tm′\n220\t\n221\t(cid:80)\n222\t\n223\tj∈m′ exp(q⊤k(m′)\n224\t\n225\tj\n226\t\n227\t) is the shared attention normalizer. For audio-video compari-\n228\t\n229\tlog\n230\t\n231\tmassa(q)\n232\tmassv(q)\n233\t\n234\t\n235\t\n236\t\n237\t\n238\t= q⊤(ca − cv) +\n239\t\n240\tlog\n241\t\n242\texp(q⊤ϵ(a)\n243\t\n244\ti\n245\t\n246\t) − log\n247\t\n248\t(cid:88)\n249\t\n250\ti∈a\n251\t\n252\t(cid:88)\n253\t\n254\tj∈v\n255\t\n256\texp(q⊤ϵ(v)\n257\tj )\n258\t\n259\t .\n260\t\n261\t(2)\n262\t\n263\tThe first term is an architectural cross-modal allocation factor induced by shared-softmax attention;\n264\tthe bracketed term aggregates within-modality residual structure. A shared top-K rule therefore\n265\tcouples allocation with token-level selection before any model-specific empirical bias is considered.\n266\t\n267\tObservation 1: shared attention is not a neutral cross-modal saliency space. Even when the\n268\tquery explicitly demands video evidence, attention still allocates more mass to audio per token.\n269\tAt L3 under video-essential (VE) queries, every pooled query token in our diagnostic satisfies\n270\tcos(q, ca) > cos(q, cv) (645/645), placing the query direction in the audio half-space. Across all 28\n271\tlayers, log(massa/massv) remains positive under both VE and audio-essential (AE) queries (Fig. 1):\n272\tquery semantics shift the ratio in the expected direction, but do not overturn it.\n273\t\n274\tNull-content controls argue that this is not merely a positional sink. Replacing the original audio\n275\twith silence or scale-matched white noise nearly removes the audio advantage; mismatched real\n276\taudio produces an intermediate shift (Appendix C.6). The shared attention is therefore responsive\n277\tto audio content, but content-responsive audio mass is not the same as query-useful audio capacity.\n278\tThis is why raw shared attention is informative yet poorly calibrated for deciding a fixed audio-video\n279\tretention budget.\n280\t\n281\tWhether the saliency signal comes from audio, video, acoustic events, or shared attention over both\n282\tmodalities, the underlying assumption is often the same: one token-level signal can decide both\n283\thow much of each modality to keep and which tokens to keep. These are different decisions. The\n284\taudio-video balance is set by q⊤(ca − cv) inside the decoder, encoder-side guides do not directly\n285\t\n286\t4\n287\t\n288\t\fFigure 4: Macer pipeline. MACER runs a single decoder prefill with staged modality-specific\n289\tpruning. It reads audio attention at ℓa and immediately applies audio retention; the decoder then\n290\tcontinues with retained audio and full video until ℓv, where video retention is applied. The final\n291\tretained audio and video tokens are merged with text tokens in the original sequence order, and the\n292\tremaining decoder layers operate on the compressed sequence.\n293\t\n294\tcontrol this term, and shared ranking does not separate it from token-level scores. A global Top-K\n295\trule turns token scores into modality budgets. Replacing the score changes which tokens rank highly,\n296\tbut still leaves the audio-video split to be induced by ranking. The token budget should be allocated\n297\tfirst, and local selection applied afterward.\n298\t\n299\tObservation 2: the audio-favoring bias is a budgeting problem, not a video-selection problem.\n300\tIn our matched-budget diagnostic, a content-aware within-video selector (ToMe-style merging) and a\n301\tcontent-blind selector (random dropping) differ by at most 0.4 pp in audio mass share at the same\n302\tvideo keep ratio, whereas reducing the video keep ratio itself increases audio’s mass share by 9.0\n303\tpp (Fig. 2b). The retained audio share also has direct accuracy consequences: a video-heavy split\n304\toutperforms an audio-heavy split by 3.1 points (Fig. 2c). Cross-modal allocation must therefore be\n305\tset at the capacity level, before within-modality selection begins.\n306\t\n307\tObservation 3: audio and video become selection-readable at different depths. Audio and\n308\tvideo residuals become legible at different layers, and any single probe depth is misaligned with at\n309\tleast one modality (Fig. 3). Video readout is layer-sensitive: earlier layers are allocation-dominated,\n310\twhile L4-L5 better expose within-video discrimination. Audio readout is comparatively flat across\n311\tshallow layers, since the audio branch does not have to exit a “how much audio at all” regime before\n312\tranking residuals. The pattern is asymmetric-an early stable audio band and a later shallow video\n313\tpreference-rather than a sharp optimum at any single layer.\n314\t\n315\tDesign constraints implied by the mechanism. Together, these observations motivate MACER’s\n316\tthree core components. Observation 1 motivates allocation-normalized modality scoring, which re-\n317\tmoves modality-mass scale before local token ranking. Observation 2 motivates capacity-coordinated\n318\tsplitting, which assigns audio/video budgets before token selection. Observation 3 motivates modality-\n319\tspecific shallow readout, using an early stable audio probe and a later shallow video probe. There-\n320\tfore, an Omnimodal compressor should separate capacity allocation, modality-local selection, and\n321\tmodality-specific readout.\n322\t\n323\t4 Method: MACER\n324\t\n325\tSection 3 shows that shared-decoder omnimodal compression is better treated as allocation before\n326\tranking. Shared attention simultaneously allocates probability mass across modalities and ranks\n327\ttokens within each modality; a shared top-K rule therefore lets the audio-favoring allocation prior\n328\tleak into token retention. MACER (Modality-Aware Capacity-Explicit Retention) implements the\n329\tallocation-before-ranking principle by separating capacity allocation from within-modality selection.\n330\tIt first assigns audio and video budgets explicitly, so the cross-modal capacity split is no longer\n331\tan accidental consequence of raw shared-attention mass. It then fills each assigned budget with\n332\tquery-conditioned tokens selected only within the corresponding modality. The actual pruning is\n333\tstaged within a single decoder prefill: audio is pruned at ℓa, and video is pruned later at ℓv.\n334\t\n335\t5\n336\t\n337\tVisionEncoderAudioEncoderUserTextQueryTimeGroup1TimeGroupnLLMText TokenVideo TokenAudio TokenUnimportant TokenStep1  Layer Readout L3 Audio LayerL5 Video LayerStep2 Audio Token Selection   Audio Top-K Retrieval Video Top-K Retrieval Step3Video Token SelectionLLM Layer 4LLM Layer 612345m………Video Local Time window saveTime Group 1Time Group 2Time Group nTime window save…\fConcretely, MACER first assigns audio and video budgets explicitly, then fills each budget using\n338\tmodality-local, allocation-normalized scores read at modality-specific shallow layers. After the split,\n339\tvideo retention uses a lightweight video-local coverage bonus as an auxiliary within-video correction\n340\tfor temporal concentration; this bonus never changes the audio–video capacity split. The pipeline is\n341\tshown in Fig. 4.\n342\t\n343\tLet Ia and Iv denote the audio and video token sets entering the LLM, with na = |Ia| and nv = |Iv|.\n344\tText tokens are always preserved; MACER compresses only multimodal prefill tokens. Let Sa and Sv\n345\tdenote the retained audio and video token sets. Given keep ratio ρ, the multimodal retention budget is\n346\t\n347\tKmm = min{na + nv, ⌊ρ(na + nv) + 0.5⌋}.\n348\t\n349\t(3)\n350\t\n351\tLet Q denote the question-token span after prompt templating, and let A(ℓ)\n352\tprobability from query token q to multimodal token i at layer ℓ and head h.\n353\t\n354\th (q, i) be the attention\n355\t\n356\tMACER is configured by modality-specific readout layers (ℓa, ℓv), a retained audio share α, and a\n357\ttemporal coverage weight λc. The audio budget fraction determines how the retained multimodal\n358\tbudget is split between audio and video, while λc weights the coverage bonus used during video-token\n359\tselection. The values used in the experiments are reported in Sec. 5.1 and Appendix A.4.\n360\t\n361\tModality-specific shallow readout. Audio and video become useful for token selection at different\n362\tshallow depths (Sec. 3). Audio saliency is already stable in early layers, whereas video saliency\n363\tbenefits from a later shallow layer before pruning. MACER therefore reads audio attention at layer ℓa\n364\tand video attention at layer ℓv, rather than forcing both modalities to share one probe depth.\n365\t\n366\tPruning is applied in modality-specific stages at the corresponding readout layers. The audio and\n367\tvideo budgets (Ka, Kv) are computed once from the original multimodal token counts before either\n368\tpruning stage. After layer ℓa is computed, MACER computes the allocation-normalized audio scores,\n369\tselects the retained audio set Sa under budget Ka, and removes the unretained audio tokens. The\n370\tdecoder then continues with all text tokens, the retained audio tokens, and the full video token set\n371\tuntil layer ℓv. After layer ℓv is computed, MACER scores video tokens, applies the video-local\n372\tcoverage rule under the video budget Kv, and removes the unretained video tokens.\n373\t\n374\tAllocation-normalized modality scoring. Raw shared attention is not a neutral token-importance\n375\tscore: it contains both a modality-level allocation factor and a within-modality selection signal\n376\t(Sec. 3). Allocation-Normalized Modality Scoring (ANMS) removes the modality-level attention\n377\tmass before ranking tokens within each modality. For modality m ∈ {a, v}, MACER normalizes\n378\teach query-to-token attention row within the target modality before averaging across query tokens\n379\tand heads:\n380\t\n381\t(cid:101)A(ℓ)\n382\t\n383\tm,h(q, i) =\n384\t\n385\tA(ℓ)\n386\t\n387\th (q, i)\n388\tA(ℓ)\n389\t\n390\th (q, j) + ε\n391\t\n392\t(cid:80)\n393\t\n394\tj∈Im\n395\t\n396\t,\n397\t\n398\ts(m)\n399\ti =\n400\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "880fc986-7020-4816-81bd-00fc9d73bafb", "uuid": "a6360d42-6ebb-480d-98fb-b4c174bc021b", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:33:15.216Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "Allocation Before Ranking: Decoupled Token\nCompression for OmniLLMs\n\nZhenghui Guo1 Yilin Yang1 Yuanbin Man2 Miao Yin2\nWeidong Shi1 Rabimba Karanjai1 Omprakash Gnawali1 Chengming Zhang1\n1Department of Computer Science, University of Houston\n2Department of Computer Science and Engineering, The University of Texas at Arlington\n\nAbstract\n\nToken compression in OmniLLMs is typically posed as a single saliency-ranking\nproblem: score each multimodal token, keep the top-K. We argue this abstraction\nis mis-specified. The same attention score simultaneously decides two things: how\nmuch retained capacity each modality receives, and which tokens within a modality\nare kept. A shared top-K rule therefore inherits this audio-favoring allocation\nprior, spending retained capacity on audio before video tokens have a chance\nto compete. We propose MACER, a training-free compressor that first assigns\nexplicit audio and video budgets, then performs allocation-normalized ranking\nwithin each modality at modality-specific shallow layers. MACER significantly\nreduces token cost while preserving accuracy across audio-grounded, audio–video\njoint, visual-dominant, and video-centric benchmarks. At 25% retention, MACER\npreserves 98.7% of full-token performance on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 97.3% on\nQwen2.5-Omni-3B. On Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, this 25% setting reaches OmniZip-\nlevel performance at 45% retention while using lower FLOPs. On OmniVinci-9B,\nthe same allocation-before-ranking principle improves over shared top-K ranking\nby up to 12.9 points.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nOmnimodal large language models (OmniLLMs) unify audio and video understanding by interleaving\nboth modalities and processing them through one decoder [Xu et al., 2025, Ye et al., 2025a, Fu et al.,\n2024, Ge et al., 2025]. This design enables a single model to jointly understand audio and video,\nbut it also makes inference scale with the combined multimodal sequence. Token compression is\ntherefore necessary. Most existing compressors treat this problem as one saliency ranking: assigning\na score to each multimodal token and keeping the top-scoring tokens under a fixed budget. We argue\nthat this ranking-only abstraction is incomplete for OmniLLM token compression.\n\nThe failure is “one score, two decisions.” In a shared decoder, attention magnitude both allocates\nprobability mass across modalities and orders tokens within each modality. A shared top-K rule\ntherefore uses the same score to decide how much audio/video capacity survives and which audio/video\ntokens survive. These are different decisions: cross-modal capacity allocation should be controlled\nbefore within-modality token selection, not induced accidentally by raw shared-attention mass. In\nOmniLLM, the coupling is concrete: shared attention exhibits a persistent, content-responsive audio\nallocation prior, so ranking audio and video tokens in one space can spend retained capacity on audio\nbefore visual evidence has a fair chance to compete.\n\nOur diagnostics give three observations that motivate the method. Observation 1: shared attention\nis not a neutral cross-modal saliency space. Even under video-essential queries, audio receives\nhigher attention mass per token across layers; null-content and distance controls show that the effect\nis content-responsive rather than a fixed positional sink. Observation 2: the audio-favoring bias\n\nPreprint.\n\narXiv:2608.01665v1  [cs.AI]  3 Aug 2026\fFigure 1: Shared attention favors audio across depth. ca and cv denote the mean audio and\nvideo keys. (a) The query q is closer to ca than to cv, so shared attention favors audio. (b) Under\nboth video-essential (VE) and audio-essential (AE) queries, log(massa/massv) stays positive across\nlayers. (c) At L3 under VE queries, pooled query positions satisfy cos(q, ca) > cos(q, cv). This\ngeometry illustrates why raw shared attention is not a neutral token-saliency space for omnimodal\ncompression.\n\nis a budgeting problem, not a video-selection problem. ToMe-style [Bolya et al., 2023] video\nmerging and random video dropping produce similar audio-mass trajectories at matched video keep\nratio, while changing the retained audio share directly changes accuracy. Observation 3: audio\nand video become selection-readable at different shallow depths. Audio readout is stable early,\nwhereas video readout is more layer-sensitive and improves around later shallow layers.\n\nWe propose MACER, a training-free inference-time compressor that instantiates this allocation-\nselection principle. MACER first assigns explicit audio and video budgets, preventing retained\ncapacity from being implicitly determined by raw shared-attention mass. It then ranks tokens only\nwithin each modality using allocation-normalized attention scores. Because audio and video saliency\nbecome readable at different shallow depths, MACER reads them at modality-specific layers before\npruning. A lightweight video-local temporal coverage term is applied only inside the assigned video\nbudget to reduce temporal over-concentration.\n\nWe evaluated MACER on three shared-decoder OmniLLM backbones (Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, Qwen2.5-\nOmni-3B, and OmniVinci-9B) across four benchmarks that span audio-grounded, audio-video joint,\nvisual-dominant, and video-centric tasks. MACER achieves the strongest aggregate performance\namong compared token-pruning baselines at every evaluated retention ratio. At 25% retention,\nMACER preserves 98.7% of full-token performance on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B (vs. 91.6% for OmniZip).\nOn Qwen2.5-Omni-3B, it preserves 97.3% (vs. 95.5% for OmniZip). On OmniVinci-9B, MACER\noutperforms shared top-K ranking by up to 12.9 points, showing that the allocation–selection\nseparation transfers across models.\n\n• We identify a structural failure of global Top-K omnimodal compression: a single importance\nscore determines both the audio-video token budget and the tokens retained within each modality.\nNo saliency signal fixes this coupling unless the token budget is allocated explicitly before\nselection.\n\n• We propose MACER, a training-free compressor that decides how many audio and video tokens to\nkeep before deciding which tokens to keep. MACER first allocates audio/video keep-ratios, then\nranks tokens within each modality using attention normalized over that modality, and reads audio\nand video saliency at different shallow layers. A small temporal-coverage rule prevents retained\nvideo tokens from clustering in time. The method requires no training, no model changes, and no\nsecond forward pass.\n\n• Our results validate that MACER preserves 98.7% / 97.3% of full-token accuracy on Qwen2.5-\nOmni-7B/3B at 25% retention, and improves over shared top-K ranking by up to 12.9 points on\nOmniVinci-9B.\n\n2\n\n\fFigure 2: Capacity allocation, not within-video selection, controls cross-modal balance. (a) A\nshared top-K rule uses one score for two decisions. (b) ToMe and random dropping differ by at most\n0.4 pp at matched video keep ratio, whereas reducing the video keep ratio itself shifts audio mass\nshare by 9.0 pp. (c) The retained audio share α affects accuracy, with a video-heavy split (α = 0.10)\noutperforming an audio-heavy split (α = 0.70) by 3.1 points.\n\n2 Related work\n\nModality competition in multimodal LLMs. Prior work has shown that MLLMs do not use\nmodalities uniformly: modality bias appears across vision, audio, time-series, and graph inputs [Wu\net al., 2025, Zheng et al., 2025], and audio-language or audio-visual analyses show that cross-modal\nattention can underuse task-relevant modalities [Wang et al., 2025]. These works mainly diagnose\nrepresentation or attention behavior. We study the deployment-time consequence for compression.\nWhen audio and video tokens share a decoder softmax under a fixed retained-token budget, imbalance\nbecomes a capacity-allocation problem: the compressor must decide both which tokens are salient\nand how much capacity each modality receives.\n\nToken compression for multimodal LLMs. Multimodal token-compression methods differ by\nwhere the retention decision is made and what signal drives it. Encoder-side methods reduce visual,\naudio-visual, or omnimodal context before or near decoder integration using encoder features, cross-\nmodal guidance, or temporal/event structure [Tao et al., 2025b, Ding et al., 2026, Li and Huang,\n2026, Guo et al., 2026, Shao et al., 2025b]. LLM-side methods instead prune or merge tokens using\nattention, redundancy, or sparsification signals inside the model [Bolya et al., 2023, Chen et al., 2024,\nShang et al., 2025, Yang et al., 2025a, Zhang et al., 2025b, Ye et al., 2025b, Xing et al., 2025, Huang\net al., 2025, Tao et al., 2025a, Jiang et al., 2025, Shao et al., 2025a], though recent VLM studies\nquestion whether text-to-visual attention alone is reliable for visual-token importance [Zhang et al.,\n2025a]. These methods provide useful saliency signals, but the audio-video budget is usually fixed,\nimplicit, or induced by a shared score. A single shared ranking therefore leaves capacity allocation\ncoupled to within-modality token selection.\n\nOmnimodal token compression. The closest omnimodal line compresses audio-video contexts\ndirectly. OmniZip [Tao et al., 2025b] uses an encoder-side audio-guided scheme before decoder\nintegration, while OmniSIFT, DASH, and FastAV explore modality-asymmetric pruning, audio-driven\nchunking, or audio-visual pruning [Ding et al., 2026, Li and Huang, 2026, Jung et al., 2026]. MACER\ndiffers along the capacity-allocation axis: it exposes the retained audio share as an explicit deployment-\nlevel control variable, then performs allocation-normalized selection within each modality using\nshallow decoder-side attention at modality-specific layers. MACER is complementary to saliency-\nsignal design: it targets a different axis, making the retained audio-video capacity split explicit before\nmodality-local token selection.\n\n3 Shared Ranking Couples Allocation and Selection\n\nShared-budget omnimodal compression is often treated as a token-ranking problem: assign a saliency\nscore to each audio and video token, then retain the top-scoring tokens under a common budget. This\nabstraction is incomplete for shared-decoder OmniLLMs. In a shared attention [Vaswani et al., 2017],\nattention magnitude does not only score token-level relevance; it also determines how probability\n\n3\n\n\fFigure 3: Audio and video become readable at different depths. We sweep one modality’s probe\nlayer while fixing the other to MACER’s default. (a) Video readout is more layer-sensitive than audio\nreadout in most settings. (b) Video readout performs best at L4-L5. (c) Audio readout remains\nrelatively stable across layers.\n\nmass is allocated across modalities. A shared ranking rule therefore contains a cross-modal allocation\nterm even when the desired decision is within-modality token selection.\n\nOne attention score, two decisions. A query’s attention to a token does two distinct things at once:\nit scores how relevant that token is within its modality, and it decides how much probability mass\ngoes to that modality versus the other (Fig. 2a). An exact decomposition makes this concrete. Let\neach key decompose as k(m)\nis the per-clip modality\ncentroid and ϵ(m)\nexact decomposition\n\nis the within-modality residual. The log attention mass on modality m admits the\n\ni = cm + ϵ(m)\n\n, where cm = 1\n|m|\n\ni∈m k(m)\n\n(cid:80)\n\ni\n\ni\n\ni\n\nlog massm(q) = q⊤cm\n(cid:124) (cid:123)(cid:122) (cid:125)\n∆alloc,m\n\n+ log\n\n(cid:88)\n\n(cid:16)\n\nexp\n\nq⊤ϵ(m)\ni\n\ni∈m\n\n(cid:124)\n\n(cid:123)(cid:122)\n∆intra,m\n\n(cid:17)\n\n(cid:125)\n\n− log Z,\n\n(1)\n\nwhere Z = (cid:80)\nson, Z cancels:\n\nm′\n\n(cid:80)\n\nj∈m′ exp(q⊤k(m′)\n\nj\n\n) is the shared attention normalizer. For audio-video compari-\n\nlog\n\nmassa(q)\nmassv(q)\n\n\n\n\n\n= q⊤(ca − cv) +\n\nlog\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(a)\n\ni\n\n) − log\n\n(cid:88)\n\ni∈a\n\n(cid:88)\n\nj∈v\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(v)\nj )\n\n .\n\n(2)\n\nThe first term is an architectural cross-modal allocation factor induced by shared-softmax attention;\nthe bracketed term aggregates within-modality residual structure. A shared top-K rule therefore\ncouples allocation with token-level selection before any model-specific empirical bias is considered.\n\nObservation 1: shared attention is not a neutral cross-modal saliency space. Even when the\nquery explicitly demands video evidence, attention still allocates more mass to audio per token.\nAt L3 under video-essential (VE) queries, every pooled query token in our diagnostic satisfies\ncos(q, ca) > cos(q, cv) (645/645), placing the query direction in the audio half-space. Across all 28\nlayers, log(massa/massv) remains positive under both VE and audio-essential (AE) queries (Fig. 1):\nquery semantics shift the ratio in the expected direction, but do not overturn it.\n\nNull-content controls argue that this is not merely a positional sink. Replacing the original audio\nwith silence or scale-matched white noise nearly removes the audio advantage; mismatched real\naudio produces an intermediate shift (Appendix C.6). The shared attention is therefore responsive\nto audio content, but content-responsive audio mass is not the same as query-useful audio capacity.\nThis is why raw shared attention is informative yet poorly calibrated for deciding a fixed audio-video\nretention budget.\n\nWhether the saliency signal comes from audio, video, acoustic events, or shared attention over both\nmodalities, the underlying assumption is often the same: one token-level signal can decide both\nhow much of each modality to keep and which tokens to keep. These are different decisions. The\naudio-video balance is set by q⊤(ca − cv) inside the decoder, encoder-side guides do not directly\n\n4\n\n\fFigure 4: Macer pipeline. MACER runs a single decoder prefill with staged modality-specific\npruning. It reads audio attention at ℓa and immediately applies audio retention; the decoder then\ncontinues with retained audio and full video until ℓv, where video retention is applied. The final\nretained audio and video tokens are merged with text tokens in the original sequence order, and the\nremaining decoder layers operate on the compressed sequence.\n\ncontrol this term, and shared ranking does not separate it from token-level scores. A global Top-K\nrule turns token scores into modality budgets. Replacing the score changes which tokens rank highly,\nbut still leaves the audio-video split to be induced by ranking. The token budget should be allocated\nfirst, and local selection applied afterward.\n\nObservation 2: the audio-favoring bias is a budgeting problem, not a video-selection problem.\nIn our matched-budget diagnostic, a content-aware within-video selector (ToMe-style merging) and a\ncontent-blind selector (random dropping) differ by at most 0.4 pp in audio mass share at the same\nvideo keep ratio, whereas reducing the video keep ratio itself increases audio’s mass share by 9.0\npp (Fig. 2b). The retained audio share also has direct accuracy consequences: a video-heavy split\noutperforms an audio-heavy split by 3.1 points (Fig. 2c). Cross-modal allocation must therefore be\nset at the capacity level, before within-modality selection begins.\n\nObservation 3: audio and video become selection-readable at different depths. Audio and\nvideo residuals become legible at different layers, and any single probe depth is misaligned with at\nleast one modality (Fig. 3). Video readout is layer-sensitive: earlier layers are allocation-dominated,\nwhile L4-L5 better expose within-video discrimination. Audio readout is comparatively flat across\nshallow layers, since the audio branch does not have to exit a “how much audio at all” regime before\nranking residuals. The pattern is asymmetric-an early stable audio band and a later shallow video\npreference-rather than a sharp optimum at any single layer.\n\nDesign constraints implied by the mechanism. Together, these observations motivate MACER’s\nthree core components. Observation 1 motivates allocation-normalized modality scoring, which re-\nmoves modality-mass scale before local token ranking. Observation 2 motivates capacity-coordinated\nsplitting, which assigns audio/video budgets before token selection. Observation 3 motivates modality-\nspecific shallow readout, using an early stable audio probe and a later shallow video probe. There-\nfore, an Omnimodal compressor should separate capacity allocation, modality-local selection, and\nmodality-specific readout.\n\n4 Method: MACER\n\nSection 3 shows that shared-decoder omnimodal compression is better treated as allocation before\nranking. Shared attention simultaneously allocates probability mass across modalities and ranks\ntokens within each modality; a shared top-K rule therefore lets the audio-favoring allocation prior\nleak into token retention. MACER (Modality-Aware Capacity-Explicit Retention) implements the\nallocation-before-ranking principle by separating capacity allocation from within-modality selection.\nIt first assigns audio and video budgets explicitly, so the cross-modal capacity split is no longer\nan accidental consequence of raw shared-attention mass. It then fills each assigned budget with\nquery-conditioned tokens selected only within the corresponding modality. The actual pruning is\nstaged within a single decoder prefill: audio is pruned at ℓa, and video is pruned later at ℓv.\n\n5\n\nVisionEncoderAudioEncoderUserTextQueryTimeGroup1TimeGroupnLLMText TokenVideo TokenAudio TokenUnimportant TokenStep1  Layer Readout L3 Audio LayerL5 Video LayerStep2 Audio Token Selection   Audio Top-K Retrieval Video Top-K Retrieval Step3Video Token SelectionLLM Layer 4LLM Layer 612345m………Video Local Time window saveTime Group 1Time Group 2Time Group nTime window save…\fConcretely, MACER first assigns audio and video budgets explicitly, then fills each budget using\nmodality-local, allocation-normalized scores read at modality-specific shallow layers. After the split,\nvideo retention uses a lightweight video-local coverage bonus as an auxiliary within-video correction\nfor temporal concentration; this bonus never changes the audio–video capacity split. The pipeline is\nshown in Fig. 4.\n\nLet Ia and Iv denote the audio and video token sets entering the LLM, with na = |Ia| and nv = |Iv|.\nText tokens are always preserved; MACER compresses only multimodal prefill tokens. Let Sa and Sv\ndenote the retained audio and video token sets. Given keep ratio ρ, the multimodal retention budget is\n\nKmm = min{na + nv, ⌊ρ(na + nv) + 0.5⌋}.\n\n(3)\n\nLet Q denote the question-token span after prompt templating, and let A(ℓ)\nprobability from query token q to multimodal token i at layer ℓ and head h.\n\nh (q, i) be the attention\n\nMACER is configured by modality-specific readout layers (ℓa, ℓv), a retained audio share α, and a\ntemporal coverage weight λc. The audio budget fraction determines how the retained multimodal\nbudget is split between audio and video, while λc weights the coverage bonus used during video-token\nselection. The values used in the experiments are reported in Sec. 5.1 and Appendix A.4.\n\nModality-specific shallow readout. Audio and video become useful for token selection at different\nshallow depths (Sec. 3). Audio saliency is already stable in early layers, whereas video saliency\nbenefits from a later shallow layer before pruning. MACER therefore reads audio attention at layer ℓa\nand video attention at layer ℓv, rather than forcing both modalities to share one probe depth.\n\nPruning is applied in modality-specific stages at the corresponding readout layers. The audio and\nvideo budgets (Ka, Kv) are computed once from the original multimodal token counts before either\npruning stage. After layer ℓa is computed, MACER computes the allocation-normalized audio scores,\nselects the retained audio set Sa under budget Ka, and removes the unretained audio tokens. The\ndecoder then continues with all text tokens, the retained audio tokens, and the full video token set\nuntil layer ℓv. After layer ℓv is computed, MACER scores video tokens, applies the video-local\ncoverage rule under the video budget Kv, and removes the unretained video tokens.\n\nAllocation-normalized modality scoring. Raw shared attention is not a neutral token-importance\nscore: it contains both a modality-level allocation factor and a within-modality selection signal\n(Sec. 3). Allocation-Normalized Modality Scoring (ANMS) removes the modality-level attention\nmass before ranking tokens within each modality. For modality m ∈ {a, v}, MACER normalizes\neach query-to-token attention row within the target modality before averaging across query tokens\nand heads:\n\n(cid:101)A(ℓ)\n\nm,h(q, i) =\n\nA(ℓ)\n\nh (q, i)\nA(ℓ)\n\nh (q, j) + ε\n\n(cid:80)\n\nj∈Im\n\n,\n\ns(m)\ni =\n", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2604}}}
