{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b334be75da1511bb", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\t\n401\t1\n402\t|Q|H\n403\t\n404\t(cid:88)\n405\t\n406\tH\n407\t(cid:88)\n408\t\n409\tq∈Q\n410\t\n411\th=1\n412\t\n413\t(cid:101)A(ℓm)\n414\t\n415\tm,h (q, i).\n416\t\n417\t(4)\n418\t\n419\tAudio scores are normalized only over Ia, and video scores only over Iv. The two ranked lists are\n420\tnever pooled into a shared audio-video top-K. ANMS decides which audio tokens compete with\n421\tother audio tokens and which video tokens compete with other video tokens; it does not decide how\n422\tmuch capacity each modality receives. The row-wise normalization removes the modality-mass\n423\tscale from each (q, h) contribution before averaging, so the final score reflects modality-local token\n424\tpreference rather than raw cross-modal attention mass.\n425\t\n426\tCapacity-coordinated split. The first role of MACER is to correct the cross-modal capacity error\n427\tcreated by the shared attention prior. If audio and video tokens are ranked together, audio can\n428\tconsume a disproportionate fraction of the retained budget before video tokens are ranked within\n429\ttheir own modality. Capacity-Coordinated Split (CCS) prevents this by assigning how many audio\n430\tand video tokens may survive before any within-modality selection is applied. This step reserves\n431\tmodality capacity; it does not yet decide which tokens are useful.\n432\t\n433\tGiven retained audio share α, we set\n434\t\n435\t¯Ka = ⌊αKmm + 0.5⌋,\n436\t\n437\t¯Kv = Kmm − ¯Ka,\n438\t\n439\t(5)\n440\t\n441\t6\n442\t\n443\t\fand then reassign unused capacity if one modality has fewer available tokens than its nominal budget:\n444\t\n445\tKa = min{na, ¯Ka + max(0, ¯Kv − nv)},\n446\t\n447\tKv = Kmm − Ka.\n448\t\n449\t(6)\n450\t\n451\tHere α is a retained-capacity share, not a raw token-count share. This distinction matters because\n452\taudio often contributes many raw tokens, while the shared attention already favors audio. Fixing\n453\tα makes the audio-video capacity split an explicit deployment choice, rather than an implicit\n454\tconsequence of raw attention magnitude or a one-way guide from another modality.\n455\t\n456\tModality-local token retention. Capacity correction is necessary but not sufficient: assigning\n457\tmore budget to video only improves the answer if the recovered video slots contain evidence that\n458\tthe decoder can use. MACER therefore fills each modality’s budget using the allocation-normalized\n459\tscores from Eq. (4). Given scores (s(a), s(v)) and budgets (Ka, Kv), audio tokens are retained by\n460\tmodality-local top-K:\n461\t\n462\tSa = TopKIdxKa {s(a)\n463\t\n464\t(7)\n465\tVideo retention follows the same modality-local principle, with a lightweight coverage bonus to\n466\tavoid concentrating all retained video tokens in the same temporal region. Let c(j) denote the fixed\n467\ttemporal partition index of video token j, obtained from the model-provided temporal position id. Let\n468\trg = |{u ∈ Sv : c(u) = g}| be the number of already retained video tokens in partition g. Starting\n469\tfrom Sv = ∅, at each greedy step we select\n470\t\n471\t: i ∈ Ia}.\n472\t\n473\ti\n474\t\n475\tj⋆ = arg max\n476\tj∈Iv\\Sv\n477\t\n478\t(cid:34)\n479\t\n480\t(cid:115)\n481\t\n482\ts(v)\n483\tj +\n484\t\n485\t(cid:35)\n486\t\n487\t,\n488\t\n489\tλc\n490\t1 + rc(j)\n491\t\n492\t(8)\n493\t\n494\tthen add j⋆ to Sv and update the corresponding partition count. The first term keeps selection\n495\tquery-conditioned, while the second provides a diminishing bonus for less-covered temporal regions.\n496\tSetting λc = 0 recovers pure video top-K.\n497\t\n498\tThis rule operates only within the assigned video budget Kv; it does not change the audio-video\n499\tcapacity split and does not use audio evidence to guide video selection. Unlike asymmetric readout,\n500\tANMS, and CCS, the coverage bonus is an auxiliary video-side correction for temporal concentration\n501\tand is ablated separately in Sec. 5.2. After selection, the retained multimodal tokens Sa ∪ Sv are\n502\tmerged with all text tokens in their original sequence order. The remaining decoder layers then\n503\toperate on this compressed sequence.\n504\t\n505\t5 Experiment Results\n506\t\n507\t5.1 Evaluation Setups and Implementation Details\n508\t\n509\tBenchmarks and baselines. We evaluate Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 3B [Xu et al., 2025] on AVUT-\n510\tBench [Yang et al., 2025b], DailyOmni [Zhou et al., 2025], WorldSense [Hong et al., 2025], and\n511\tVideo-MME [Fu et al., 2025], covering audio-grounded, audio-video joint, visual-dominant, and\n512\tvideo-centric regimes. We compare against representative baselines under a unified Qwen2.5-\n513\tOmni decoder-side token-accounting protocol: Random retention as a content-blind lower bound;\n514\tFastV [Chen et al., 2024], an LMM-internal shared-attention ranker; and OmniZip [Tao et al., 2025b],\n515\tthe closest executable encoder-side audio-guided omnimodal compressor under our setting. To\n516\ttest whether MACER transfers beyond the Qwen2.5-Omni family, we additionally evaluate it on\n517\tOmniVinci-9B [Ye et al., 2025a] against shared top-K ranking on AVUTBench, DailyOmni, and\n518\tWorldSense; full results are in Appendix F.\n519\t\n520\tImplementation and deployment configurations. We implement MACER on Qwen2.5-Omni-\n521\t7B/3B and OmniVinci-9B using NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada 48GB GPUs. Text tokens are always\n522\tpreserved; compression is applied only to decoder-side multimodal prefill tokens. We report perfor-\n523\tmance and inference cost at three retention rates, ρ ∈ {25%, 35%, 45%}. Decoding is greedy and\n524\tFlashAttention-2 [Dao, 2023] is enabled throughout. MACER exposes the audio-video allocation as\n525\tan explicit deployment variable. We use (ℓa, ℓv, α, λc) = (3, 5, 0.30, 0.20) for Qwen2.5-Omni-7B\n526\tand (4, 7, 0.32, 0.30) for Qwen2.5-Omni-3B. These settings are selected once on a held-out split\n527\tdisjoint from all evaluation sets, then frozen across benchmarks, query types, and retention ratios.\n528\tAppendix D.4 shows that this choice lies in a stable allocation band: allowing a per-benchmark oracle\n529\tallocation improves the normalized average by only 0.92 percentage points.\n530\t\n531\t7\n532\t\n533\t\fTable 1: Pareto comparison on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. At 25% retention, MACER forms a lower-\n534\tFLOPs operating point than OmniZip at 45%.\n535\t\n536\tMethod\n537\t\n538\tRetain\n539\t\n540\tFLOPs AVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Avg(3)\n541\t\n542\tOmniZip\n543\tMACER (Ours)\n544\t\n545\t45%\n546\t25%\n547\t\n548\t39.2% 63.07\n549\t34.8% 63.67\n550\t\n551\t∆\n552\t\n553\t-20pp\n554\t\n555\t-4.4pp\n556\t\n557\t+0.60\n558\t\n559\t59.82\n560\t62.07\n561\t\n562\t+2.25\n563\t\n564\t44.61\n565\t44.80\n566\t\n567\t+0.19\n568\t\n569\t55.83\n570\t56.85\n571\t\n572\t+1.02\n573\t\n574\t5.2 Main Results\n575\t\n576\tAll benchmarks are run through LMMs-Eval [Zhang et al., 2024] on the full evaluation sets under a\n577\tunified protocol. Table 1 reports the headline efficiency comparison; Table 2 then provides the full\n578\tretention-controlled benchmark results.\n579\t\n580\tPareto comparison. Table 1 shows the headline efficiency result. At 25% retention, MACER uses\n581\t4.4pp fewer FLOPs than OmniZip at 45% while improving Avg(3) by 1.02 points, with consistent\n582\tper-benchmark gains on AVUTBench (+0.60), DailyOmni (+2.25), and WorldSense (+0.19).\n583\t\n584\tTable 2: Comparison on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B across audio-grounded, audio-video joint, visual-\n585\tdominant, and video-centric benchmarks. The best result among token-pruning methods within\n586\teach retained-ratio block (25%/35%/45%) is in bold, and the second-best is underlined. FastV† is\n587\tevaluated on H100 due to OOM on Ada6000 under the long multimodal context; its average excludes\n588\tVideo-MME, denoted by “-”.\n589\t\n590\tMethod\n591\t\n592\tSettings\n59