sary but not sufficient: assigning\nmore budget to video only improves the answer if the recovered video slots contain evidence that\nthe decoder can use. MACER therefore fills each modality’s budget using the allocation-normalized\nscores from Eq. (4). Given scores (s(a), s(v)) and budgets (Ka, Kv), audio tokens are retained by\nmodality-local top-K:\n\nSa = TopKIdxKa {s(a)\n\n(7)\nVideo retention follows the same modality-local principle, with a lightweight coverage bonus to\navoid concentrating all retained video tokens in the same temporal region. Let c(j) denote the fixed\ntemporal partition index of video token j, obtained from the model-provided temporal position id. Let\nrg = |{u ∈ Sv : c(u) = g}| be the number of already retained video tokens in partition g. Starting\nfrom Sv = ∅, at each greedy step we select\n\n: i ∈ Ia}.\n\ni\n\nj⋆ = arg max\nj∈Iv\\Sv\n\n(cid:34)\n\n(cid:115)\n\ns(v)\nj +\n\n(cid:35)\n\n,\n\nλc\n1 + rc(j)\n\n(8)\n\nthen add j⋆ to Sv and update the corresponding partition count. The first term keeps selection\nquery-conditioned, while the second provides a diminishing bonus for less-covered temporal regions.\nSetting λc = 0 recovers pure video top-K.\n\nThis rule operates only within the assigned video budget Kv; it does not change the audio-video\ncapacity split and does not use audio evidence to guide video selection. Unlike asymmetric readout,\nANMS, and CCS, the coverage bonus is an auxiliary video-side correction for temporal concentration\nand is ablated separately in Sec. 5.2. After selection, the retained multimodal tokens Sa ∪ Sv are\nmerged with all text tokens in their original sequence order. The remaining decoder layers then\noperate on this compressed sequence.\n\n5 Experiment Results\n\n5.1 Evaluation Setups and Implementation Details\n\nBenchmarks and baselines. We evaluate Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 3B [Xu et al., 2025] on AVUT-\nBench [Yang et al., 2025b], DailyOmni [Zhou et al., 2025], WorldSense [Hong et al., 2025], and\nVideo-MME [Fu et al., 2025], covering audio-grounded, audio-video joint, visual-dominant, and\nvideo-centric regimes. We compare against representative baselines under a unified Qwen2.5-\nOmni decoder-side token-accounting protocol: Random retention as a content-blind lower bound;\nFastV [Chen et al., 2024], an LMM-internal shared-attention ranker; and OmniZip [Tao et al., 2025b],\nthe closest executable encoder-side audio-guided omnimodal compressor under our setting. To\ntest whether MACER transfers beyond the Qwen2.5-Omni family, we additionally evaluate it on\nOmniVinci-9B [Ye et al., 2025a] against shared top-K ranking on AVUTBench, DailyOmni, and\nWorldSense; full results are in Appendix F.\n\nImplementation and deployment configurations. We implement MACER on Qwen2.5-Omni-\n7B/3B and OmniVinci-9B using NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada 48GB GPUs. Text tokens are always\npreserved; compression is applied only to decoder-side multimodal prefill tokens. We report perfor-\nmance and inference cost at three retention rates, ρ ∈ {25%, 35%, 45%}. Decoding is greedy and\nFlashAttention-2 [Dao, 2023] is enabled throughout. MACER exposes the audio-video allocation as\nan explicit deployment variable. We use (ℓa, ℓv, α, λc) = (3, 5, 0.30, 0.20) for Qwen2.5-Omni-7B\nand (4, 7, 0.32, 0.30) for Qwen2.5-Omni-3B. These settings are selected once on a held-out split\ndisjoint from all evaluation sets, then frozen across benchmarks, query types, and retention ratios.\nAppendix D.4 shows that this choice lies in a stable allocation band: allowing a per-benchmark oracle\nallocation improves the normalized average by only 0.92 percentage points.\n\n7\n\n\fTable 1: Pareto comparison on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. At 25% retention, MACER forms a lower-\nFLOPs operating point than OmniZip at 45%.\n\nMethod\n\nRetain\n\nFLOPs AVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Avg(3)\n\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\n\n45%\n25%\n\n39.2% 63.07\n34.8% 63.67\n\n∆\n\n-20pp\n\n-4.4pp\n\n+0.60\n\n59.82\n62.07\n\n+2.25\n\n44.61\n44.80\n\n+0.19\n\n55.83\n56.85\n\n+1.02\n\n5.2 Main Results\n\nAll benchmarks are run through LMMs-Eval [Zhang et al., 2024] on the full evaluation sets under a\nunified protocol. Table 1 reports the headline efficiency comparison; Table 2 then provides the full\nretention-controlled benchmark results.\n\nPareto comparison. Table 1 shows the headline efficiency result. At 25% retention, MACER uses\n4.4pp fewer FLOPs than OmniZip at 45% while improving Avg(3) by 1.02 points, with consistent\nper-benchmark gains on AVUTBench (+0.60), DailyOmni (+2.25), and WorldSense (+0.19).\n\nTable 2: Comparison on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B across audio-grounded, audio-video joint, visual-\ndominant, and video-centric benchmarks. The best result among token-pruning methods within\neach retained-ratio block (25%/35%/45%) is in bold, and the second-best is underlined. FastV† is\nevaluated on H100 due to OOM on Ada6000 under the long multimodal context; its average excludes\nVideo-MME, denoted by “-”.\n\nMethod\n\nSettings\n\nAVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Video-MME\n\nNorm. Avg.\n\nRetained Ratio\n\nFLOPs Ratio\n\nAvg.\n\nAvg.\n\nAvg.\n\nAvg.\n\nFull Tokens\nRandom\nFastV†\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\n\nFull Tokens\nRandom\nFastV†\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\nOmniZip\nMACER (Ours)\n\n100%\n55%\n50%\n45%\n45%\n35%\n35%\n25%\n25%\n\n100%\n55%\n50%\n45%\n45%\n35%\n35%\n25%\n25%\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n\n64.5\n61.0\n58.4\n63.07\n64.72\n61.05\n64.63\n59.10\n63.67\n\n62.1\n59.2\n59.8\n59.82\n63.32\n59.40\n62.32\n57.39\n62.07\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-3B\n\n62.2\n58.7\n55.9\n61.30\n62.13\n60.10\n60.52\n58.90\n59.74\n\n60.2\n57.4\n58.0\n59.73\n59.15\n58.06\n58.15\n56.47\n58.40\n\n100%\n49.2%\n54.1%\n39.2%\n50.1%\n29.6%\n42.2%\n20.6%\n34.8%\n\n100%\n46.1%\n49.2%\n36.1%\n48.5%\n26.8%\n41.0%\n18.3%\n34.1%\n\n46.8\n43.6\n44.3\n44.61\n44.00\n43.98\n45.05\n38.18\n44.80\n\n46.4\n42.8\n44.4\n45.20\n46.45\n44.30\n45.55\n43.40\n44.24\n\n66.0\n65.4\n-\n66.3\n66.9\n65.7\n66.2\n66.5\n66.2\n\n62.6\n61.1\n-\n62.80\n63.21\n62.70\n63.37\n62.60\n63.01\n\n100%\n95.5%\n93.8%\n97.5%\n99.4%\n96.0%\n99.3%\n91.6%\n98.7%\n\n100%\n94.9%\n94.0%\n98.9%\n99.8%\n97.2%\n98.3%\n95.5%\n97.3%\n\nRepresentative baseline comparison. Table 2 shows broad matched-retention gains over compared\ntoken-pruning baselines, especially under aggressive compression. On Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, MACER\nbeats OmniZip in 10 of 12 matched benchmark-ratio cells; at 25% retention, the gains reach +4.57 on\nAVUTBench, +4.68 on DailyOmni, and +6.62 on WorldSense, with Video-MME preserved at full-\ntoken level. On Qwen2.5-Omni-3B, MACER wins 11 of 12 matched benchmark-ratio cells, including\nall four benchmarks at 25% retention. On OmniVinci-9B, MACER leads shared top-K ranking by up\nto 12.9 points (Appendix F). A matched-retention comparison with concurrent DASH [Li and Huang,\n2026] appears in Table 16. This widening-gap pattern reflects the same failure mode across baselines:\nRandom has no importance signal, FastV applies global Top-K to decoder attention, and OmniZip\nuses an encoder-side audio-guided score but still lets the retained composition be induced by pruning.\nMACER allocates the audio-video token budget before selection.\n\n8\n\n\fTable 3: End-to-end inference efficiency on WorldSense on a single NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU.\nFastV runs out of memory under the long context.\n\nMethod\n\nρ\n\nFLOPs Acc. ↑\n\nTotal Lat. ↓\n\nPrefill ↓ Decode ↓ Mem. ↓", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 2604}}}
