{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8bff00e848ae9d04", "type": "tool_result", "content": "800\t\n801\tFull Tokens\n802\tFastV\n803\t\n804\t-\n805\t\n806\t100%\n807\t50% 54.1%\n808\t\n809\t46.80\n810\t\n811\t10.5s\n812\t\n813\tOmniZip\n814\tMACER\n815\t\n816\t45% 39.2% 44.61\n817\t25% 34.8% 44.80\n818\t\n819\t9.54s\n820\t9.17s\n821\t\n822\t∼3.70s\n823\tOOM\n824\t\n825\t2.38s\n826\t2.53s\n827\t\n828\t∼6.8s\n829\t\n830\t41G\n831\t\n832\t7.16s\n833\t6.64s\n834\t\n835\t29G\n836\t25G\n837\t\n838\tTable 4: Ablation study on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at 25% retention. Retained A/V is the benchmark-\n839\tmacro retained multimodal composition, excluding text. Coverage operates only within the assigned\n840\tvideo budget and does not change the audio/video split.\n841\t\n842\tVariant\n843\t\n844\tRetained A/V\n845\t\n846\tAVUT\n847\t\n848\tDailyOmni\n849\t\n850\tWorldSense\n851\t\n852\tAvg.\n853\t\n854\tShared top-K ranking\n855\tMACER core, no coverage\n856\tSingle readout layer\n857\tMACER full\n858\t\n859\t57.6 / 42.4\n860\t30.0 / 70.0\n861\t30.0 / 70.0\n862\t30.0 / 70.0\n863\t\n864\t61.27 (60.0/40.0) 59.57 (56.7/43.3) 43.60 (56.0/44.0) 54.81\n865\t56.54\n866\t54.38\n867\t56.85\n868\t\n869\t61.82\n870\t60.87\n871\t62.07\n872\t\n873\t63.33\n874\t57.47\n875\t63.67\n876\t\n877\t44.47\n878\t44.80\n879\t44.80\n880\t\n881\tEfficiency analyses. Table 3 confirms the Pareto efficiency advantage end-to-end. MACER com-\n882\tpresses 20pp more aggressively than OmniZip (retaining only 25% vs. 45% of multimodal tokens),\n883\tyet simultaneously achieves higher accuracy (+0.19), lower total latency (-0.37s), lower decoding\n884\tcost (-0.52s), and lower peak memory (-4G), while using 4.4pp fewer FLOPs. MACER therefore\n885\tPareto-dominates OmniZip on accuracy, latency, memory, and compute, while retaining far fewer\n886\ttokens.\n887\t\n888\tAblation study. Table 4 isolates the contribution of each design component. Shared top-K ranking\n889\tcouples allocation with selection, collapsing into a 57.6/42.4 audio/video split and capping accuracy\n890\tat 54.81. Replacing it with MACER’s explicit capacity-coordinated split, modality-local scoring, and\n891\tasymmetric readout fixes the budget at 30.0/70.0 and lifts accuracy to 56.54 (+1.73), showing that\n892\tthe main gain comes from decoupling allocation from selection. Adding the video-local coverage\n893\tcorrection keeps the same budget and further raises accuracy to 56.85 (+0.31; +2.04 over shared\n894\ttop-K), indicating that coverage is a secondary within-video correction.\n895\t\n896\t6 Limitations\n897\t\n898\tMACER is designed for audio-video OmniLLMs that process audio and video tokens with a shared\n899\tdecoder. Extending the allocation-selection decomposition to models with substantially different\n900\tmultimodal fusion designs remains future work. The method uses shallow decoder-side attention\n901\treadout before pruning, which enables query-conditioned modality-local scoring. At the evaluated\n902\toperating points, its latency and memory profile remains favorable in our end-to-end measurements\n903\t(Table 3). Broader modality combinations and additional backbone families are natural directions for\n904\tfollow-up work.\n905\t\n906\t7 Conclusion\n907\t\n908\tOmnimodal token compression should not be reduced to a single shared-ranking problem. In audio-\n909\tvideo OmniLLMs that place both modalities in the same LLM-side attention stream, shared attention\n910\tcouples cross-modal capacity allocation with within-modality token selection, while audio and video\n911\tbecome selection-readable at different shallow depths. MACER instantiates an allocation-before-\n912\tranking principle by assigning audio-video capacity explicitly, ranking tokens locally within each\n913\tmodality, and reading saliency at modality-specific shallow layers, all without retraining. The results\n914\ton Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B and OmniVinci-9B support this decomposition as a design interface for\n915\tfuture omnimodal compressors, where allocation, selection, and readout can be improved separately\n916\trather than collapsed into one shared score.\n917\t\n918\t9\n919\t\n920\t\fReferences\n921\t\n922\tDaniel Bolya, Cheng-Yang Fu, Xiaoliang Dai, Peizhao Zhang, Christoph Feichtenhofer, and Judy Hoffman.\n923\tToken merging: Your ViT but faster. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations,\n924\t2023. URL https://openreview.net/forum?id=JroZRaRw7Eu.\n925\t\n926\tLiang Chen, Haozhe Zhao, Tianyu Liu, Shuai Bai, Junyang Lin, Chang Zhou, and Baobao Chang. An image is\n927\tworth 1/2 tokens after layer 2: Plug-and-play inference acceleration for large vision-language models. In\n928\tComputer Vision – ECCV 2024, pages 19–35. Springer, 2024. doi: 10.1007/978-3-031-73004-7_2. URL\n929\thttps://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73004-7_2.\n930\t\n931\tTri Dao. Flashattention-2: Faster attention with better parallelism and work partitioning. arXiv preprint\n932\t\n933\tarXiv:2307.08691, 2023.\n934\t\n935\tYue Ding, Yiyan Ji, Jungang Li, Xuyang Liu, Xinlong Chen, Junfei Wu, Bozhou Li, Bohan Zeng, Yang\n936\tShi, Yushuo Guan, Yuanxing Zhang, Jiaheng Liu, Qiang Liu, Pengfei Wan, and Liang Wang. OmniSIFT:\n937\tModality-asymmetric token compression for efficient omni-modal large language models. arXiv preprint\n938\tarXiv:2602.04804, 2026. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2602.04804. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04804.\n939\t\n940\tChaoyou Fu, Haojia Lin, Zuwei Long, Yunhang Shen, Yuhang Dai, Meng Zhao, Yi-Fan Zhang, Shaoqi Dong,\n941\tYangze Li, Xiong Wang, et al. Vita: Towards open-source interactive omni multimodal llm. arXiv preprint\n942\tarXiv:2408.05211, 2024.\n943\t\n944\tChaoyou Fu, Yuhan Dai, Yongdong Luo, Lei Li, Shuhuai Ren, Renrui Zhang, Zihan Wang, Chenyu Zhou,\n945\tYunhang Shen, Mengdan Zhang, Peixian Chen, Yanwei Li, Shaohui Lin, Sirui Zhao, Ke Li, Tong\n946\tXu, Xiawu Zheng, Enhong Chen, Caifeng Shan, Ran He, and Xing Sun. 