main-paper claims to appendix sections. The appendix follows the main paper’s\n1109\tscale-specific MACER settings.\n1110\t\n1111\tMain-paper claim\n1112\t\n1113\tAppendix\n1114\t\n1115\tExtended content\n1116\t\n1117\tExperimental setup and re-\n1118\tproducibility\n1119\tMACER implementation\n1120\t\n1121\tallocation\n1122\t\n1123\tShared ranking couples allo-\n1124\tcation and selection\n1125\tis\n1126\tAudio\n1127\tquery-robust and content-\n1128\tconditioned\n1129\tSingle-guide saliency does\n1130\tnot directly control alloca-\n1131\ttion\n1132\tAudio/video readout is asym-\n1133\tmetric\n1134\tExplicit capacity split and\n1135\tvideo coverage\n1136\t\n1137\tEfficiency reporting\n1138\t\n1139\tCross-backbone support\n1140\t\n1141\tA\n1142\t\n1143\tB\n1144\t\n1145\tC\n1146\t\n1147\tC\n1148\t\n1149\tC\n1150\t\n1151\tD\n1152\t\n1153\tD\n1154\t\n1155\tE\n1156\t\n1157\tF\n1158\t\n1159\tBenchmark taxonomy, preprocessing, diagnostic\n1160\tsetup, calibration split, token statistics\n1161\tExact algorithm, allocation-normalized scoring, ex-\n1162\tplicit capacity split, video coverage rule\n1163\tRatio decomposition, token-count term, Q-centroid\n1164\tdiagnostic, packing and distance controls\n1165\tPer-layer attention statistics, frame-count sanity\n1166\tcheck, visualized silence/noise/mismatched-audio\n1167\tcontrols\n1168\tToMe/random invariance, allocation-vs-ranking\n1169\tstress test, and concise encoder-side guide analy-\n1170\tsis\n1171\tCentered readout sweeps, full 5×5 heatmaps, sensi-\n1172\ttivity ratios\n1173\tRetained-share α robustness, temporal concentra-\n1174\ttion, and matched DASH comparison; main Table 4\n1175\treports the component ablation\n1176\tFLOPs-ratio estimates used for the efficiency tables;\n1177\tmain Table 3 reports measured latency\n1178\tOmniVinci-9B transfer under native packing and\n1179\treused 7B readout\n1180\t\n1181\tTable 6: Main evaluation-suite taxonomy. Grouping by modality demand helps interpret benchmark-\n1182\tdependent compression behavior, but is not used by MACER during inference.\n1183\t\n1184\tBenchmark\n1185\t\n1186\tModality demand\n1187\t\n1188\tTemporal regime\n1189\t\n1190\tRole\n1191\t\n1192\tAVUTBench\n1193\tDailyOmni\n1194\t\n1195\tAudio-grounded\n1196\tAV-joint\n1197\t\n1198\tMixed\n1199\tShort clips\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "880fc986-7020-4816-81bd-00fc9d73bafb", "uuid": "8f453ae2-6992-47ea-9847-f65129d8e203", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:33:31.400Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\nFull Tokens\nFastV\n\n-\n\n100%\n50% 54.1%\n\n46.80\n\n10.5s\n\nOmniZip\nMACER\n\n45% 39.2% 44.61\n25% 34.8% 44.80\n\n9.54s\n9.17s\n\n∼3.70s\nOOM\n\n2.38s\n2.53s\n\n∼6.8s\n\n41G\n\n7.16s\n6.64s\n\n29G\n25G\n\nTable 4: Ablation study on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at 25% retention. Retained A/V is the benchmark-\nmacro retained multimodal composition, excluding text. Coverage operates only within the assigned\nvideo budget and does not change the audio/video split.\n\nVariant\n\nRetained A/V\n\nAVUT\n\nDailyOmni\n\nWorldSense\n\nAvg.\n\nShared top-K ranking\nMACER core, no coverage\nSingle readout layer\nMACER full\n\n57.6 / 42.4\n30.0 / 70.0\n30.0 / 70.0\n30.0 / 70.0\n\n61.27 (60.0/40.0) 59.57 (56.7/43.3) 43.60 (56.0/44.0) 54.81\n56.54\n54.38\n56.85\n\n61.82\n60.87\n62.07\n\n63.33\n57.47\n63.67\n\n44.47\n44.80\n44.80\n\nEfficiency analyses. Table 3 confirms the Pareto efficiency advantage end-to-end. MACER com-\npresses 20pp more aggressively than OmniZip (retaining only 25% vs. 45% of multimodal tokens),\nyet simultaneously achieves higher accuracy (+0.19), lower total latency (-0.37s), lower decoding\ncost (-0.52s), and lower peak memory (-4G), while using 4.4pp fewer FLOPs. MACER therefore\nPareto-dominates OmniZip on accuracy, latency, memory, and compute, while retaining far fewer\ntokens.\n\nAblation study. Table 4 isolates the contribution of each design component. Shared top-K ranking\ncouples allocation with selection, collapsing into a 57.6/42.4 audio/video split and capping accuracy\nat 54.81. Replacing it with MACER’s explicit capacity-coordinated split, modality-local scoring, and\nasymmetric readout fixes the budget at 30.0/70.0 and lifts accuracy to 56.54 (+1.73), showing that\nthe main gain comes from decoupling allocation from selection. Adding the video-local coverage\ncorrection keeps the same budget and further raises accuracy to 56.85 (+0.31; +2.04 over shared\ntop-K), indicating that coverage is a secondary within-video correction.\n\n6 Limitations\n\nMACER is designed for audio-video OmniLLMs that process audio and video tokens with a shared\ndecoder. Extending the allocation-selection decomposition to models with substantially different\nmultimodal fusion designs remains future work. The method uses shallow decoder-side attention\nreadout before pruning, which enables query-conditioned modality-local scoring. At the evaluated\noperating points, its latency and memory profile remains favorable in our end-to-end measurements\n(Table 3). Broader modality combinations and additional backbone families are natural directions for\nfollow-up work.\n\n7 Conclusion\n\nOmnimodal token compression should not be reduced to a single shared-ranking problem. In audio-\nvideo OmniLLMs that place both modalities in the same LLM-side attention stream, shared attention\ncouples cross-modal capacity allocation with within-modality token selection, while audio and video\nbecome selection-readable at different shallow depths. MACER instantiates an allocation-before-\nranking principle by assigning audio-video capacity explicitly, ranking tokens locally within each\nmodality, and reading saliency at modality-specific shallow layers, all without retraining. The results\non Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B and OmniVinci-9B support this decomposition as a design interface for\nfuture omnimodal compressors, where allocation, selection, and readout can be improved separately\nrather than collapsed into one shared score.\n\n9\n\n\fReferences\n\nDaniel Bolya, Cheng-Yang Fu, Xiaoliang Dai, Peizhao Zhang, Christoph Feichtenhofer, and Judy Hoffman.\nToken merging: Your ViT but faster. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations,\n2023. URL https://openreview.net/forum?id=JroZRaRw7Eu.\n\nLiang Chen, Haozhe Zhao, Tianyu Liu, Shuai Bai, Junyang Lin, Chang Zhou, and Baobao Chang. An image is\nworth 1/2 tokens after layer 2: Plug-and-play inference acceleration for large vision-language models. In\nComputer Vision – ECCV 2024, pages 19–35. Springer, 2024. doi: 10.1007/978-3-031-73004-7_2. URL\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73004-7_2.\n\nTri Dao. Flashattention-2: Faster attention with better parallelism and work partitioning. arXiv preprint\n\narXiv:2307.08691, 2023.\n\nYue Ding, Yiyan Ji, Jungang Li, Xuyang Liu, Xinlong Chen, Junfei Wu, Bozhou Li, Bohan Zeng, Yang\nShi, Yushuo Guan, Yuanxing Zhang, Jiaheng Liu, Qiang Liu, Pengfei Wan, and Liang Wang. OmniSIFT:\nModality-asymmetric token compression for efficient omni-modal large language models. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2602.04804, 2026. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2602.04804. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04804.\n\nChaoyou Fu, Haojia Lin, Zuwei Long, Yunhang Shen, Yuhang Dai, Meng Zhao, Yi-Fan Zhang, Shaoqi Dong,\nYangze Li, Xiong Wang, et al. Vita: Towards open-source interactive omni multimodal llm. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2408.05211, 2024.\n\nChaoyou Fu, Yuhan Dai, Yongdong Luo, Lei Li, Shuhuai Ren, Renrui Zhang, Zihan Wang, Chenyu Zhou,\nYunhang Shen, Mengdan Zhang, Peixian Chen, Yanwei Li, Shaohui Lin, Sirui Zhao, Ke Li, Tong\nXu, Xiawu Zheng, Enhong Che