{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-82be02fb9fc4a1fc", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t\n1201\tWorldSense\n1202\t\n1203\tVideo-MME\n1204\t\n1205\tVisual-dominant,\n1206\taugmented\n1207\tVideo-centric\n1208\t\n1209\taudio-\n1210\t\n1211\tMixed/longer\n1212\t\n1213\tShort/med/long\n1214\t\n1215\tVideo-side anchor\n1216\t\n1217\tAudio allocation sensitivity\n1218\tCross-modal grounding / event\n1219\tsequence\n1220\tMixed-modality robustness\n1221\t\n1222\tA Experimental Protocol and Reproducibility\n1223\t\n1224\tA.1 Benchmarks and evaluation protocol\n1225\t\n1226\tWe group benchmarks by the modality structure of answer-relevant evidence rather than by dataset\n1227\tprovenance. The grouping is used only to organize analysis; all MACER configurations are fixed\n1228\tacross benchmarks.\n1229\t\n1230\t13\n1231\t\n1232\t\fAVUTBench contains audio-grounded questions where the audio stream carries primary answer\n1233\tevidence and the video stream often supplies scene context. DailyOmni emphasizes audio–video\n1234\tevent alignment, temporal ordering, and cross-modal grounding. WorldSense is mostly visually\n1235\tdriven but includes non-trivial audio-augmented cases. Video-MME, evaluated in the no-subtitle\n1236\tsetting, provides a video-centric anchor for testing whether an omnimodal compressor remains\n1237\tcompetitive when the primary evidence is visual.\n1238\t\n1239\tWe evaluate Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and Qwen2.5-Omni-3B under fixed preprocessing and decoding\n1240\tsettings for each benchmark. Text tokens are always preserved. Compression is applied only to multi-\n1241\tmodal prefill tokens. Reported average scores are benchmark-normalized against the corresponding\n1242\tfull-token model, so FULL is 100% for each benchmark before averaging.\n1243\t\n1244\tA.2 Model preprocessing and token statistics\n1245\t\n1246\tWe distinguish dataset-level frame caps from decoder-side token counts. Dataset preprocessing\n1247\tfollows the benchmark protocols used in the main paper: Video-MME permits up to 768 raw frames\n1248\tand WorldSense up to 128 raw frames before model-side sampling. MACER itself operates only\n1249\ton the audio and video tokens that enter the LLM after the model preprocessing pipeline. Unless\n1250\ta benchmark-specific protocol explicitly changes the model sampler, the default Qwen2.5-Omni\n1251\tvideo preprocessing uses 12 sampled frames, yielding nv = 864 decoder-side video tokens. Audio\n1252\ttoken counts vary with clip duration; under the default preprocessing the median audio token count is\n1253\tapproximately na ≈ 1460.\n1254\t\n1255\tThis token-count imbalance is distinct from the retained capacity split. A setting such as α = 0.30\n1256\tintentionally allocates fewer retained tokens to audio than its raw token share, counterbalancing the\n1257\taudio-favoring allocation prior observed in the shared attention space. All keep ratios and retained\n1258\tbudgets are computed from decoder-side multimodal token counts, not from raw frame caps.\n1259\t\n1260\tA.3 Mechanism diagnostic setup\n1261\t\n1262\tThe mechanism analyses use Qwen2.5-Omni-7B on paired audio–video examples. For paired video-\n1263\tessential (VE) and audio-essential (AE) diagnostics, each clip is associated with a VE query and an\n1264\tAE query when available. Attention statistics are computed from question-text tokens after prompt\n1265\ttemplating, excluding system tokens, BOS tokens, and the answer prefix. Unless otherwise stated,\n1266\tattention is averaged over heads within each layer before computing modality-level summaries.\n1267\t\n1268\tFor the main mechanism study, we use N = 500 paired examples. The layer-cell diagnostic in\n1269\tAppendix C.4 and the null-content controls in Appendix C.6 use a smaller paired subset of N = 30\n1270\tclips because those controls require paired interventions over original, silence, white-noise, and\n1271\tmismatched-audio variants. Readout-layer sweeps evaluate downstream accuracy under the same\n1272\tcompression budget while varying audio and video probe layers.\n1273\t\n1274\tA.4 Fixed default selection\n1275\t\n1276\tMACER uses fixed backbone-level defaults rather than benchmark-specific or query-specific hy-\n1277\tperparameters. We use a held-out development split of approximately 100 clips, disjoint from all\n1278\treported evaluation sets and balanced across the modality-demand categories in Table 6, to finalize\n1279\tthe backbone-level operating point. Once selected, the configuration is frozen across all reported\n1280\tbenchmarks, query types, and keep ratios within that model scale. No model weights are updated.\n1281\t\n1282\tTable 7 reports the scale-specific defaults used in the main paper. The readout layers follow the\n1283\tshallow-readout diagnostics: audio is read from an early stable layer and video from a later shallow\n1284\tlayer. Qwen2.5-Omni-7B uses (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), while Qwen2.5-Omni-3B uses (ℓa, ℓv) = (4, 7).\n1285\tThese settings are frozen within scale; no benchmark-specific or query-specific tuning is used for the\n1286\treported results.\n1287\t\n1288\tThe retained audio share α is treated as a fixed retained-capacity setting, not a raw audio-token share\n1289\tand not a per-example allocation policy. We use α = 0.30 for 7B and α = 0.32 for 3B. These values\n1290\tinstantiate the same design rule—make the audio–video capacity split explicit rather than letting\n1291\tshared attention determine it implicitly—but we do not assume that one numerical value transfers\n1292\tunchanged across model scales. For video coverage, we use C = 8 coarse temporal chunks and\n1293\t\n1294\t14\n1295\t\n1296\t\fscale-specific coverage strengths λc = 0.20 for 7B and λc = 0.30 for 3B. All quantities in Table 7\n1297\tare fixed before the reported benchmark runs. Appendix D.4 further audits the 7B retained-audio\n1298\tshare and shows that the frozen α = 0.30 setting lies in a stable operating band rather than requiring\n1299\tbenchmark-specific allocation tuning.\n1300\t\n1301\tTable 7: Scale-specific MACER defaults used after held-out calibration. The settings follow the main\n1302\tpaper: they are frozen within each model scale, but not shared across 7B and 3B.\n1303\t\n1304\tQuantity\n1305\t\n1306\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n1307\t\n1308\tQwen2.5-Omni-3B\n1309\t\n1310\tAudio readout / pruning after ℓa\n1311\tVideo readout / pruning after ℓv\n1312\tFLOPs proxy depth Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv)\n1313\tRetained audio share α\n1314\tVideo chunks C\n1315\tCoverage strength λc\n1316\tText-token handling\n1317\tModel weights\n1318\tForward passes\n1319\t\n1320\t3\n1321\t5\n1322\t5\n1323\t0.30\n1324\t8\n1325\t0.20\n1326\t\n1327\t4\n1328\t7\n1329\t7\n1330\t0.32\n1331\t8\n1332\t0.30\n1333\t\n1334\tAlways preserved\n1335\tFrozen\n1336\tSingle prefill pass; cached readout rows\n1337\t\n1338\tA.5 Metrics and statistical tests\n1339\t\n1340\tMain benchmark tables report raw benchmark accuracy and the normalized cross-benchmark average.\n1341\tFor each benchmark, normalized accuracy is computed relative to the full-token model under the\n1342\tsame model scale and evaluation protocol:\n1343\t\n1344\tNormAvg(m) =\n1345\t\n1346\t100\n1347\t|B|\n1348\t\n1349\t(cid:88)\n1350\t\n1351\tb∈B\n1352\t\n1353\tAccm,b\n1354\tAccFull,b\n1355\t\n1356\t.\n1357\t\n1358\t(9)\n1359\t\n1360\tTable 8 summarizes the resulting single-forward-pass MACER procedure.\n1361\t\n1362\tTable 8: MACER algorithm. The procedure separates cross-modal capacity coordination from\n1363\twithin-modality token selection. The readout layers, retained audio share, and coverage strength are\n1364\tscale-specific values from Table 7.\n1365\t\n1366\t1\n1367\t\n1368\t2\n1369\t\n1370\t3\n1371\t\n1372\t4\n1373\t\n1374\t5\n1375\t\n1376\t6\n1377\t\n1378\t7\n1379\t\n1380\tSet the total multimodal budget Kmm = min{na +nv, ⌊ρ(na +nv)+0.5⌋} and allocate audio/video\n1381\tbudgets (Ka, Kv) using the calibrated retained audio share α.\n1382\tRun the decoder through layer ℓa on the full multimodal prefill and cache query-span attention rows\n1383\tfor audio.\n1384\tCompute allocation-normalized audio scores s(a) using Eq. (10); select audio tokens by modality-\n1385\tlocal top-K, Sa = topKa {s(a)\n1386\tContinue the same forward pass with all text tokens, retained audio tokens, and full video tokens\n1387\tuntil layer ℓv; cache query-span attention rows for video.\n1388\tCompute allocation-normalized video scores s(v), partition video tokens into C coarse chunks, and\n1389\tinitialize S(0)\n1390\tRun the score-aware chunk-coverage greedy rule in Eq. (14) for Kv steps to obtain Sv. Setting\n1391\tλc = 0 recovers pure video top-K.\n1392\tContinue the remaining decoder blocks on text tokens plus Sa ∪ Sv in the original sequence order.\n1393\t\n1394\t: i ∈ Ia}, and prune unretained audio tokens.\n1395\t\n1396\tv = ∅ with chunk counts n(0)\n1397\t\n1398\tc = 0.\n1399\t\n1400\ti\n1401\t\n1402\tMechanism intervention tests use paired sign tests unless otherwise stated. For null-content controls,\n1403\tthe paired unit is the clip, and the tested quantity is the direction of the shift in mid-layer log(v/a)\n1404\trelative to the original-audio condition.\n1405\t\n1406\tMain accuracy tables are deterministic evaluations under fixed preprocessing, fixed model weights,\n1407\tfixed decoding, and fixed hyperparameters. We do not attach error bars to all benchmark cells unless\n1408\trepeated full evaluations or paired per-example prediction files are available. For stochastic baselines\n1409\tsuch as random token dropping, the random seed is fixed when computing the reported table cells.\n1410\tCode and calibrated configurations will be released upon publication.\n1411\t\n1412\t15\n1413\t\n1414\t\fB MACER Implementation Details\n1415\t\n1416\tB.1 Allocation-normalized modality scoring\n1417\t\n1418\tRaw shared attention is not a neutral token-importance score because it contains both a modality-level\n1419\tallocation factor and a within-modality selection signal. Allocation-Normalized Modality Scoring\n1420\t(ANMS) removes the modality-level attention mass before ranking tokens within each modality.\n1421\tFor modality m ∈ {a, v}, MACER normalizes each query-to-token attention row within the target\n1422\tmodality before averaging across query tokens and heads:\n1423\t\n1424\t(cid:101)A(ℓ)\n1425\t\n1426\tm,h(q, i) =\n1427\t\n1428\tA(ℓ)\n1429\t\n1430\th (q, i)\n1431\tA(ℓ)\n1432\t\n1433\th (q, j) + ε\n1434\t\n1435\t(cid:80)\n1436\t\n1437\tj∈Im\n1438\t\n1439\t,\n1440\t\n1441\ts(m)\n1442\ti =\n1443\t\n1444\t1\n1445\t|Q|H\n1446\t\n1447\t(cid:88)\n1448\t\n1449\tH\n1450\t(cid:88)\n1451\t\n1452\tq∈Q\n1453\t\n1454\th=1\n1455\t\n1456\t(cid:101)A(ℓm)\n1457\t\n1458\tm,h (q, i).\n1459\t\n1460\t(10)\n1461\t\n1462\tAudio scores are normalized only over Ia, and video scores only over Iv. The two ranked lists are\n1463\tnever pooled into a shared audio–video top-K. ANMS decides which audio tokens compete with\n1464\tother audio tokens and which video tokens compete with other video tokens; it does not decide how\n1465\tmuch capacity each modality receives. We compute the normalization denominator in fp32 with a\n1466\tsmall positive ε for numerical stability.\n1467\t\n1468\tB.2 Capacity-coordinated split\n1469\t\n1470\tGiven keep ratio ρ, the multimodal budget is\n1471\t\n1472\tKmm = min{na + nv, ⌊ρ(na + nv) + 0.5⌋}.\n1473\t\n1474\tGiven retained audio share α, we set\n1475\t\n1476\t¯Ka = ⌊αKmm + 0.5⌋,\n1477\t\n1478\t¯Kv = Kmm − ¯Ka,\n1479\t\n1480\t(11)\n1481\t\n1482\t(12)\n1483\t\n1484\tand reassign unused capacity if one modality has fewer available tokens than its nominal budget:\n1485\t\n1486\tKa = min{na, ¯Ka + max(0, ¯Kv − nv)},\n1487\t\n1488\tKv = Kmm − Ka.\n1489\t\n1490\t(13)\n1491\t\n1492\tHere α is a retained-capacity share, not a raw token-count share. This prevents the audio–video\n1493\tbudget from being implicitly determined by raw shared-attention magnitude or by a one-way guide\n1494\tfrom another modality.\n1495\t\n1496\tB.3 Video score-aware chunk-coverage greedy\n1497\t\n1498\tThe video coverage rule is applied only after the video budget Kv has been assigned by the capacity\n1499\tsplit. It does not change the cross-modal capacity split and does not use audio evidence. Its role is\n1500\tlimited to reducing over-concentration in video token selection while keeping the selection score-\n1501\taware.\n1502\t\n1503\tWe divide video tokens into C coarse temporal chunks, using the model’s native temporal grouping\n1504\twhen available and equal-size token bins otherwise. Let c(j) ∈ {1, . . . , C} denote the chunk index of\n1505\tvideo token j. Given the assigned video budget Kv, initialize S(0)\n1506\tc = 0 for all chunks.\n1507\tAt greedy step r = 1, . . . , Kv, select\n1508\t\n1509\tv = ∅ and n(0)\n1510\t\n1511\tjr = arg max\n1512\t\n1513\tj∈Iv\\S(r−1)\n1514\t\n1515\tv\n1516\t\n1517\t\n1518\ts(v)\n1519\t\n1520\tj +\n1521\t\n1522\t(cid:118)\n1523\t(cid:117)\n1524\t(cid:117)\n1525\t(cid:116)\n1526\t\n1527\t\n1528\t\n1529\t ,\n1530\t\n1531\tλc\n1532\t1 + n(r−1)\n1533\t\n1534\tc(j)\n1535\t\n1536\tthen update\n1537\t\n1538\tv = S(r−1)\n1539\tS(r)\n1540\t\n1541\tv\n1542\t\n1543\t∪ {jr},\n1544\t\n1545\tc(jr) = n(r−1)\n1546\tn(r)\n1547\t\n1548\tc(jr) + 1,\n1549\t\n1550\t(14)\n1551\t\n1552\t(15)\n1553\t\n1554\twith all other chunk counts unchanged. The final retained video set is Sv = S(Kv)\n1555\t. The first term in\n1556\tEq. (14) preserves query-conditioned video saliency. The second term gives under-covered chunks a\n1557\tλc, and after n retained tokens from the same\n1558\tdiminishing bonus: an empty chunk receives bonus\n1559\tchunk the bonus becomes (cid:112)λc/(1 + n). Large score gaps are still decided by s(v)\n1560\t; the coverage\n1561\tterm mainly biases near-ties toward chunks with fewer retained tokens.\n1562\t\n1563\t√\n1564\t\n1565\tv\n1566\t\n1567\tj\n1568\t\n1569\t16\n1570\t\n1571\t\fB.4 Text-token preservation and single-forward-pass staged pruning\n1572\t\n1573\tText tokens are always preserved. MACER applies pruning in two modality-specific stages within\n1574\ta single forward prefill. After layer ℓa, audio scores s(a) are computed and only the selected audio\n1575\ttokens Sa are kept; all text tokens and all video tokens remain active. After layer ℓv, video scores\n1576\ts(v) are computed on the current sequence, the video budget Kv is applied with the video-local\n1577\tcoverage rule, and only the selected video tokens Sv are kept. After the second pruning stage, the\n1578\tactive sequence contains all text tokens plus Sa ∪ Sv in the original relative order.\n1579\t\n1580\tConsequently, the 7B configuration prunes audio after L3 and video after L5, while the 3B con-\n1581\tfiguration prunes audio after L4 and video after L7. This is a single-forward-pass implementation\n1582\twith staged pruning, not a single-shot pruning step after Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv). In practice, the score in\n1583\tEq. (10) is averaged over the last K = 4 tokens of the question span Q rather than the full Q. This\n1584\trestriction is applied identically to both the audio readout at ℓa and the video readout at ℓv.\n1585\t\n1586\tC Mechanism Diagnostics\n1587\t\n1588\tC.1 Allocation–selection decomposition and token-count control\n1589\t\n1590\tFor a query token q, decompose each modality-m key as\n1591\t\n1592\ti = cm + ϵ(m)\n1593\tk(m)\n1594\t\n1595\ti\n1596\t\n1597\t,\n1598\t\n1599\t(16)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "880fc986-7020-4816-81bd-00fc9d73bafb", "uuid": "553a48ad-02d3-4323-9dde-b5dbd7796513", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:33:39.845Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\nWorldSense\n\nVideo-MME\n\nVisual-dominant,\naugmented\nVideo-centric\n\naudio-\n\nMixed/longer\n\nShort/med/long\n\nVideo-side anchor\n\nAudio allocation sensitivity\nCross-modal grounding / event\nsequence\nMixed-modality robustness\n\nA Experimental Protocol and Reproducibility\n\nA.1 Benchmarks and evaluation protocol\n\nWe group benchmarks by the modality structure of answer-relevant evidence rather than by dataset\nprovenance. The grouping is used only to organize analysis; all MACER configurations are fixed\nacross benchmarks.\n\n13\n\n\fAVUTBench contains audio-grounded questions where the audio stream carries primary answer\nevidence and the video stream often supplies scene context. DailyOmni emphasizes audio–video\nevent alignment, temporal ordering, and cross-modal grounding. WorldSense is mostly visually\ndriven but includes non-trivial audio-augmented cases. Video-MME, evaluated in the no-subtitle\nsetting, provides a video-centric anchor for testing whether an omnimodal compressor remains\ncompetitive when the primary evidence is visual.\n\nWe evaluate Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and Qwen2.5-Omni-3B under fixed preprocessing and decoding\nsettings for each benchmark. Text tokens are always preserved. Compression is applied only to multi-\nmodal prefill tokens. Reported average scores are benchmark-normalized against the corresponding\nfull-token model, so FULL is 100% for each benchmark before averaging.\n\nA.2 Model preprocessing and token statistics\n\nWe distinguish dataset-level frame caps from decoder-side token counts. Dataset preprocessing\nfollows the benchmark protocols used in the main paper: Video-MME permits up to 768 raw frames\nand WorldSense up to 128 raw frames before model-side sampling. MACER itself operates only\non the audio and video tokens that enter the LLM after the model preprocessing pipeline. Unless\na benchmark-specific protocol explicitly changes the model sampler, the default Qwen2.5-Omni\nvideo preprocessing uses 12 sampled frames, yielding nv = 864 decoder-side video tokens. Audio\ntoken counts vary with clip duration; under the default preprocessing the median audio token count is\napproximately na ≈ 1460.\n\nThis token-count imbalance is distinct from the retained capacity split. A setting such as α = 0.30\nintentionally allocates fewer retained tokens to audio than its raw token share, counterbalancing the\naudio-favoring allocation prior observed in the shared attention space. All keep ratios and retained\nbudgets are computed from decoder-side multimodal token counts, not from raw frame caps.\n\nA.3 Mechanism diagnostic setup\n\nThe mechanism analyses use Qwen2.5-Omni-7B on paired audio–video examples. For paired video-\nessential (VE) and audio-essential (AE) diagnostics, each clip is associated with a VE query and an\nAE query when available. Attention statistics are computed from question-text tokens after prompt\ntemplating, excluding system tokens, BOS tokens, and the answer prefix. Unless otherwise stated,\nattention is averaged over heads within each layer before computing modality-level summaries.\n\nFor the main mechanism study, we use N = 500 paired examples. The layer-cell diagnostic in\nAppendix C.4 and the null-content controls in Appendix C.6 use a smaller paired subset of N = 30\nclips because those controls require paired interventions over original, silence, white-noise, and\nmismatched-audio variants. Readout-layer sweeps evaluate downstream accuracy under the same\ncompression budget while varying audio and video probe layers.\n\nA.4 Fixed default selection\n\nMACER uses fixed backbone-level defaults rather than benchmark-specific or query-specific hy-\nperparameters. We use a held-out development split of approximately 100 clips, disjoint from all\nreported evaluation sets and balanced across the modality-demand categories in Table 6, to finalize\nthe backbone-level operating point. Once selected, the configuration is frozen across all reported\nbenchmarks, query types, and keep ratios within that model scale. No model weights are updated.\n\nTable 7 reports the scale-specific defaults used in the main paper. The readout layers follow the\nshallow-readout diagnostics: audio is read from an early stable layer and video from a later shallow\nlayer. Qwen2.5-Omni-7B uses (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), while Qwen2.5-Omni-3B uses (ℓa, ℓv) = (4, 7).\nThese settings are frozen within scale; no benchmark-specific or query-specific tuning is used for the\nreported results.\n\nThe retained audio share α is treated as a fixed retained-capacity setting, not a raw audio-token share\nand not a per-example allocation policy. We use α = 0.30 for 7B and α = 0.32 for 3B. These values\ninstantiate the same design rule—make the audio–video capacity split explicit rather than letting\nshared attention determine it implicitly—but we do not assume that one numerical value transfers\nunchanged across model scales. For video coverage, we use C = 8 coarse temporal chunks and\n\n14\n\n\fscale-specific coverage strengths λc = 0.20 for 7B and λc = 0.30 for 3B. All quantities in Table 7\nare fixed before the reported benchmark runs. Appendix D.4 further audits the 7B retained-audio\nshare and shows that the frozen α = 0.30 setting lies in a stable operating band rather than requiring\nbenchmark-specific allocation tuning.\n\nTable 7: Scale-specific MACER defaults used after held-out calibration. The settings follow the main\npaper: they are frozen within each model scale, but not shared across 7B and 3B.\n\nQuantity\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-3B\n\nAudio readout / pruning after ℓa\nVideo readout / pruning after ℓv\nFLOPs proxy depth Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv)\nRetained audio share α\nVideo chunks C\nCoverage strength λc\nText-token handling\nModel weights\nForward passes\n\n3\n5\n5\n0.30\n8\n0.20\n\n4\n7\n7\n0.32\n8\n0.30\n\nAlways preserved\nFrozen\nSingle prefill pass; cached readout rows\n\nA.5 Metrics and statistical tests\n\nMain benchmark tables report raw benchmark accuracy and the normalized cross-benchmark average.\nFor each benchmark, normalized accuracy is computed relative to the full-token model under the\nsame model scale and evaluation protocol:\n\nNormAvg(m) =\n\n100\n|B|\n\n(cid:88)\n\nb∈B\n\nAccm,b\nAccFull,b\n\n.\n\n(9)\n\nTable 8 summarizes the resulting single-forward-pass MACER procedure.\n\nTable 8: MACER algorithm. The procedure separates cross-modal capacity coordination from\nwithin-modality token selection. The readout layers, retained audio share, and coverage strength are\nscale-specific values from Table 7.\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\n5\n\n6\n\n7\n\nSet the total multimodal budget Kmm = min{na +nv, ⌊ρ(na +nv)+0.5⌋} and allocate audio/video\nbudgets (Ka, Kv) using the calibrated retained audio share α.\nRun the decoder through layer ℓa on the full multimodal prefill and cache query-span attention rows\nfor audio.\nCompute allocation-normalized audio scores s(a) using Eq. (10); select audio tokens by modality-\nlocal top-K, Sa = topKa {s(a)\nContinue the same forward pass with all text tokens, retained audio tokens, and full video tokens\nuntil layer ℓv; cache query-span attention rows for video.\nCompute allocation-normalized video scores s(v), partition video tokens into C coarse chunks, and\ninitialize S(0)\nRun the score-aware chunk-coverage greedy rule in Eq. (14) for Kv steps to obtain Sv. Setting\nλc = 0 recovers pure video top-K.\nContinue the remaining decoder blocks on text tokens plus Sa ∪ Sv in the original sequence order.\n\n: i ∈ Ia}, and prune unretained audio tokens.\n\nv = ∅ with chunk counts n(0)\n\nc = 0.\n\ni\n\nMechanism intervention tests use paired sign tests unless otherwise stated. For null-content controls,\nthe paired unit is the clip, and the tested quantity is the direction of the shift in mid-layer log(v/a)\nrelative to the original-audio condition.\n\nMain accuracy tables are deterministic evaluations under fixed preprocessing, fixed model weights,\nfixed decoding, and fixed hyperparameters. We do not attach error bars to all benchmark cells unless\nrepeated full evaluations or paired per-example prediction files are available. For stochastic baselines\nsuch as random token dropping, the random seed is fixed when computing the reported table cells.\nCode and calibrated configurations will be released upon publication.\n\n15\n\n\fB MACER Implementation Details\n\nB.1 Allocation-normalized modality scoring\n\nRaw shared attention is not a neutral token-importance score because it contains both a modality-level\nallocation factor and a within-modality selection signal. Allocation-Normalized Modality Scoring\n(ANMS) removes the modality-level attention mass before ranking tokens within each modality.\nFor modality m ∈ {a, v}, MACER normalizes each query-to-token attention row within the target\nmodality before averaging across query tokens and heads:\n\n(cid:101)A(ℓ)\n\nm,h(q, i) =\n\nA(ℓ)\n\nh (q, i)\nA(ℓ)\n\nh (q, j) + ε\n\n(cid:80)\n\nj∈Im\n\n,\n\ns(m)\ni =\n\n1\n|Q|H\n\n(cid:88)\n\nH\n(cid:88)\n\nq∈Q\n\nh=1\n\n(cid:101)A(ℓm)\n\nm,h (q, i).\n\n(10)\n\nAudio scores are normalized only over Ia, and video scores only over Iv. The two ranked lists are\nnever pooled into a shared audio–video top-K. ANMS decides which audio tokens compete with\nother audio tokens and which video tokens compete with other video tokens; it does not decide how\nmuch capacity each modality receives. We compute the normalization denominator in fp32 with a\nsmall positive ε for numerical stability.\n\nB.2 Capacity-coordinated split\n\nGiven keep ratio ρ, the multimodal budget is\n\nKmm = min{na + nv, ⌊ρ(na + nv) + 0.5⌋}.\n\nGiven retained audio share α, we set\n\n¯Ka = ⌊αKmm + 0.5⌋,\n\n¯Kv = Kmm − ¯Ka,\n\n(11)\n\n(12)\n\nand reassign unused capacity if one modality has fewer available tokens than its nominal budget:\n\nKa = min{na, ¯Ka + max(0, ¯Kv − nv)},\n\nKv = Kmm − Ka.\n\n(13)\n\nHere α is a retained-capacity share, not a raw token-count share. This prevents the audio–video\nbudget from being implicitly determined by raw shared-attention magnitude or by a one-way guide\nfrom another modality.\n\nB.3 Video score-aware chunk-coverage greedy\n\nThe video coverage rule is applied only after the video budget Kv has been assigned by the capacity\nsplit. It does not change the cross-modal capacity split and does not use audio evidence. Its role is\nlimited to reducing over-concentration in video token selection while keeping the selection score-\naware.\n\nWe divide video tokens into C coarse temporal chunks, using the model’s native temporal grouping\nwhen available and equal-size token bins otherwise. Let c(j) ∈ {1, . . . , C} denote the chunk index of\nvideo token j. Given the assigned video budget Kv, initialize S(0)\nc = 0 for all chunks.\nAt greedy step r = 1, . . . , Kv, select\n\nv = ∅ and n(0)\n\njr = arg max\n\nj∈Iv\\S(r−1)\n\nv\n\n\ns(v)\n\nj +\n\n(cid:118)\n(cid:117)\n(cid:117)\n(cid:116)\n\n\n\n ,\n\nλc\n1 + n(r−1)\n\nc(j)\n\nthen update\n\nv = S(r−1)\nS(r)\n\nv\n\n∪ {jr},\n\nc(jr) = n(r−1)\nn(r)\n\nc(jr) + 1,\n\n(14)\n\n(15)\n\nwith all other chunk counts unchanged. The final retained video set is Sv = S(Kv)\n. The first term in\nEq. (14) preserves query-conditioned video saliency. The second term gives under-covered chunks a\nλc, and after n retained tokens from the same\ndiminishing bonus: an empty chunk receives bonus\nchunk the bonus becomes (cid:112)λc/(1 + n). Large score gaps are still decided by s(v)\n; the coverage\nterm mainly biases near-ties toward chunks with fewer retained tokens.\n\n√\n\nv\n\nj\n\n16\n\n\fB.4 Text-token preservation and single-forward-pass staged pruning\n\nText tokens are always preserved. MACER applies pruning in two modality-specific stages within\na single forward prefill. After layer ℓa, audio scores s(a) are computed and only the selected audio\ntokens Sa are kept; all text tokens and all video tokens remain active. After layer ℓv, video scores\ns(v) are computed on the current sequence, the video budget Kv is applied with the video-local\ncoverage rule, and only the selected video tokens Sv are kept. After the second pruning stage, the\nactive sequence contains all text tokens plus Sa ∪ Sv in the original relative order.\n\nConsequently, the 7B configuration prunes audio after L3 and video after L5, while the 3B con-\nfiguration prunes audio after L4 and video after L7. This is a single-forward-pass implementation\nwith staged pruning, not a single-shot pruning step after Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv). In practice, the score in\nEq. (10) is averaged over the last K = 4 tokens of the question span Q rather than the full Q. This\nrestriction is applied identically to both the audio readout at ℓa and the video readout at ℓv.\n\nC Mechanism Diagnostics\n\nC.1 Allocation–selection decomposition and token-count control\n\nFor a query token q, decompose each modality-m key as\n\ni = cm + ϵ(m)\nk(m)\n\ni\n\n,\n\n(16)", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1200, "totalLines": 2604}}}
