{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9fc48980dc35fa6c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1600\t\n1601\twhere cm is the per-clip modality centroid and ϵ(m)\n1602\tis the within-modality residual. The usual\n1603\tattention scaling factor is absorbed into q. For audio–video comparison, the shared softmax normalizer\n1604\tcancels:\n1605\t\n1606\ti\n1607\t\n1608\tlog\n1609\t\n1610\tmassa(q)\n1611\tmassv(q)\n1612\t\n1613\t\n1614\t\n1615\t\n1616\t\n1617\t= q⊤(ca − cv) +\n1618\t\n1619\tlog\n1620\t\n1621\texp(q⊤ϵ(a)\n1622\t\n1623\ti\n1624\t\n1625\t) − log\n1626\t\n1627\t(cid:88)\n1628\t\n1629\ti∈a\n1630\t\n1631\t(cid:88)\n1632\t\n1633\tj∈v\n1634\t\n1635\texp(q⊤ϵ(v)\n1636\tj )\n1637\t\n1638\t .\n1639\t\n1640\t(17)\n1641\t\n1642\tThe first term is the cross-modal allocation term; the bracketed term is the difference between within-\n1643\tmodality residual aggregation terms. The bracketed term is not a pure token-relevance term because\n1644\tit also contains token-count and residual-concentration effects. The main point is that raw shared\n1645\tattention necessarily mixes a cross-modal mass term with modality-local variation, while a shared\n1646\ttop-K rule uses the mixed quantity for token retention.\n1647\t\n1648\tThe residual aggregation term contains a token-count component:\n1649\t\n1650\tlog\n1651\t\n1652\t(cid:88)\n1653\t\n1654\ti∈m\n1655\t\n1656\texp(q⊤ϵ(m)\n1657\t\n1658\ti\n1659\t\n1660\t) = log nm + log\n1661\t\n1662\t(cid:32)\n1663\t\n1664\t1\n1665\tnm\n1666\t\n1667\t(cid:88)\n1668\t\n1669\ti∈m\n1670\t\n1671\t(cid:33)\n1672\t\n1673\texp(q⊤ϵ(m)\n1674\t\n1675\ti\n1676\t\n1677\t)\n1678\t\n1679\t.\n1680\t\n1681\t(18)\n1682\t\n1683\tThis separates token count from residual concentration. Under the default 12-frame setting, video\n1684\tcontributes nv = 864 tokens, while audio has median na ≈ 1460. Therefore, the audio-favoring\n1685\tattention pattern is not a consequence of video having more raw tokens. Conversely, MACER’s\n1686\tretained capacity split deliberately under-samples audio relative to its raw token share to preserve\n1687\tsufficient video evidence under aggressive pruning.\n1688\t\n1689\tC.2 Q-centroid mechanism\n1690\t\n1691\tEquation (17) identifies q⊤(ca − cv) as the cross-modal allocation term. We therefore test whether\n1692\tthe observed audio prior is mainly driven by query-side alignment with the modality centroids, rather\n1693\tthan by key-side geometry alone or by within-audio coherence. Figure 5 reports three complementary\n1694\tdiagnostics: interventions on the query direction, layer-wise query-centroid alignment, and the\n1695\tpersistence of the same bias at the terminal decode position.\n1696\t\n1697\t17\n1698\t\n1699\t\fFigure 5: Q-centroid mechanism behind the audio prior. (a) Query-side interventions show\n1700\tthat audio mass is strongly controlled by alignment between the query direction and the modality\n1701\tcentroids; key-side geometry and within-audio coherence controls alone do not explain the prior. (b)\n1702\tAcross layers, the query representation is directionally closer to the audio centroid than to the video\n1703\tcentroid. (c) The same Q-side audio bias is visible at the decode terminal position, indicating that the\n1704\tallocation tendency is not limited to the averaged query-token span.\n1705\t\n1706\tThis diagnostic is consistent with the decomposition: the observed behavior is not merely that audio\n1707\tkeys are numerous or locally coherent, but that the shared decoder places query directions in an audio-\n1708\tfavoring centroid geometry. This is why raw shared attention can remain a useful content-responsive\n1709\tsignal while still being a poor cross-modal capacity allocator.\n1710\t\n1711\tC.3 Packing structure and distance-matched controls\n1712\t\n1713\tTo rule out a simple prefix-sink explanation, we inspect the input packing order. The observed\n1714\tstructure is\n1715\t\n1716\t[system/user/template] → ⟨vision_bos⟩ ⟨audio_bos⟩ → V288 → A50 → V288 → A50 →\n1717\tV288 → Arest → [query]\n1718\t\n1719\tAudio is interleaved with video blocks rather than placed as a pure prefix. The largest audio block\n1720\tappears close to the query, motivating the distance-matched control below.\n1721\t\n1722\tWe control for token distance to the query using quantile-binned non-parametric analysis. Table 9\n1723\treports the equal-distance audio–video ratio at selected layers. Early layers show that distance\n1724\texplains a large part of the apparent audio advantage; mid-to-late layers retain an audio-favoring\n1725\tresidual even after distance matching.\n1726\t\n1727\tTable 9: Distance-matched analysis. At early layers, matching token distance largely removes the\n1728\taudio advantage. Starting in the mid stack, a residual audio advantage persists after controlling for\n1729\tdistance, indicating that the effect is not only a recency artifact.\n1730\t\n1731\tLayer\n1732\t\n1733\tEqual-distance log(v/a)\n1734\t\n1735\tv/a ratio\n1736\t\n1737\tInterpretation\n1738\t\n1739\tL7\n1740\tL10\n1741\tL14\n1742\tL19\n1743\t\n1744\t−0.012\n1745\t+0.007\n1746\t−0.725\n1747\t−0.886\n1748\t\n1749\t0.988\n1750\t1.007\n1751\t0.484\n1752\t0.412\n1753\t\n1754\tPositional bias dominates\n1755\tEqual-distance parity\n1756\tAudio residual emerges\n1757\tAudio residual remains stable\n1758\t\n1759\tDistance matching and null-content ablation probe different confounds: distance matching controls\n1760\ttoken position while keeping original content, whereas mismatched-audio control disrupts semantic\n1761\taudio–video coupling while keeping a real audio stream. At L14, the two estimates are numerically\n1762\tclose: distance-matched analysis gives log(v/a) = −0.725, while the mismatched-audio control\n1763\tgives log(v/a) = −0.746. The close agreement between the distance-matched and mismatched-\n1764\taudio estimates supports the same qualitative conclusion: the mid-layer residual is not explained by\n1765\teither position or semantic alignment alone.\n1766\t\n1767\tWe also fit log-linear regressions\n1768\t\n1769\tlog(attention) = β log(distance) + ϵ\n1770\t\n1771\t18\n1772\t\n1773\t\fper layer. The R2 values range from 0.000 to 0.036, indicating that attention–distance relationships\n1774\tare not well described by a simple power law. The quantile-binned non-parametric analysis above is\n1775\tused for this reason and does not rely on the regression model being well specified.\n1776\t\n1777\tC.4 Per-layer attention statistics\n1778\t\n1779\tTable 10 reports per-token attention statistics across all 28 decoder layers on the paired-query\n1780\tdiagnostic subset (30 clips × 2 query types × 28 layers, head-aggregated, 1680 cells total).\n1781\t\n1782\tTable 10: Per-layer audio–video attention statistics on selected layers. Video wins the per-token\n1783\tattention comparison in only 8.8% of layer–clip–query cells pooled across query types and layers.\n1784\tv > a rate\n1785\t\n1786\tlog(v/a) median\n1787\t\n1788\tInterpretation\n1789\t\n1790\tLayer\n1791\t\n1792\tL0\n1793\tL3\n1794\tL7\n1795\tL9\n1796\tL10\n1797\tL14\n1798\tL19\n1799\tL27\n1800\t\n1801\tPooled\n1802\t\n1803\t−3.02\n1804\t−2.14\n1805\t−0.73\n1806\t−0.31\n1807\t−0.42\n1808\t−0.89\n1809\t−1.03\n1810\t−0.96\n1811\t\n1812\t−0.96\n1813\t\n1814\t0/60\n1815\t0/60\n1816\t10/60\n1817\t13/60\n1818\t12/60\n1819\t7/60\n1820\t9/60\n1821\t9/60\n1822\t\n1823\tStrong early positional bias\n1824\tStrong early positional bias\n1825\tPositional effect decaying\n1826\tWeakest audio bias\n1827\tTransition boundary\n1828\tAudio residual emerging\n1829\tAudio residual established\n1830\tFinal layer\n1831\t\n1832\t148/1680 (8.8%)\n1833\t\n1834\tUnder video-essential queries, video wins in 12.1% of cells (102/840). Under audio-essential queries,\n1835\tvideo wins in 5.5% of cells (46/840). The query shifts the ratio systematically in the predicted\n1836\tdirection, but does not reliably remove the baseline preference for audio. This supports the main-\n1837\tpaper phrasing “query-robust” rather than the stronger and less precise “query-invariant.”\n1838\t\n1839\tC.5 Frame-count diagnostic\n1840\t\n1841\tTo check whether the 12-frame default creates the audio advantage, we vary the video frame count\n1842\ton three paired clips. Table 11 shows that the v/a per-token ratio monotonically decreases with\n1843\tmore video tokens in this small diagnostic, suggesting that the 12-frame default is not unusually\n1844\tunfavorable to video in this diagnostic.\n1845\t\n1846\tTable 11: Frame-count diagnostic. The v/a ratio decreases monotonically as frame count increases\n1847\ton the three tested paired clips, indicating that the 12-frame default is not obviously unfavorable to\n1848\tvideo among the tested settings. Higher frame counts were OOM on our hardware.\n1849\t\n1850\tFrames\n1851\t\n1852\tnv\n1853\t\n1854\tv/a ratio Monotonic pairs\n1855\t\n1856\tSign flips\n1857\t\n1858\t12\n1859\t16\n1860\t20\n1861\t\n1862\t864\n1863\t1152\n1864\t1440\n1865\t\n1866\t0.215\n1867\t0.200\n1868\t0.179\n1869\t\n1870\t–\n1871\t3/3 vs. 12\n1872\t3/3 vs. 16\n1873\t\n1874\t–\n1875\t0\n1876\t0\n1877\t\n1878\tC.6 Null-content controls\n1879\t\n1880\tWe replace original audio with silence, scale-matched white noise, or mismatched real audio. Silence\n1881\tand white noise nearly remove the audio advantage, while mismatched audio produces an intermediate\n1882\tshift. This graded response shows that audio attention is not an input-independent sink: the shared\n1883\tattention space is responsive to audio content. Content responsiveness, however, does not make\n1884\traw attention magnitude a calibrated capacity signal under compression. Table 12 reports the\n1885\tcorresponding shifts in mid-layer log(v/a) relative to the original-audio condition, and Figure 6\n1886\tvisualizes the same null-content and distance-matched controls.\n1887\t\n1888\t19\n1889\t\n1890\t\fTable 12: Shift in mid-layer log(v/a) relative to original audio across 30 paired clips. Silence and\n1891\twhite noise produce large positive shifts, while mismatched audio produces a smaller but still positive\n1892\tshift.\n1893\t\n1894\tCondition\n1895\t\n1896\tMean shift Median shift\n1897\t\n1898\tMin\n1899\t\n1900\tMax\n1901\t\n1902\tSilence\n1903\tWhite noise\n1904\tMismatched\n1905\t\n1906\t+1.83\n1907\t+1.60\n1908\t+0.52\n1909\t\n1910\t+1.80\n1911\t+1.55\n1912\t+0.48\n1913\t\n1914\t+1.17 +2.81\n1915\t+0.81 +2.49\n1916\t−0.04 +1.66\n1917\t\n1918\tFigure 6: Null-content and distance-matched controls for the audio prior. (a) Replacing original\n1919\taudio with mismatched real audio, scale-matched white noise, or silence shifts log(v/a) toward\n1920\tvideo; the prior therefore responds to audio content rather than behaving as an input-independent\n1921\tsink. (b) Equal-distance matching removes much of the early positional effect, but a mid-layer\n1922\taudio-favoring residual remains. (c) At L14, the mismatched-audio and distance-matched controls\n1923\tgive nearly the same residual estimate, supporting the conclusion that neither recency nor semantic\n1924\taudio–video alignment alone explains the mid-layer effect.\n1925\t\n1926\tSign tests against the null hypothesis of zero shift give silence (30/30 pairs, p = 9.3 × 10−10), white\n1927\tnoise (30/30, p = 9.3 × 10−10), and mismatched audio (29/30, p = 2.9 × 10−8). These tests support\n1928\tthe direction of the shift, and the tabled means and medians summarize the paired intervention subset.\n1929\t\n1930\tThe original-to-mismatched shift (+0.52) reflects loss of semantic video–audio alignment while\n1931\tpreserving acoustic structure. The mismatched-to-white-noise shift (+1.08) reflects destruction of\n1932\tsemantic audio content. The white-noise-to-silence shift (+0.23) reflects the residual contribution\n1933\tof acoustic energy versus complete absence. The graded collapse supports a content-conditioned\n1934\tinterpretation rather than a pure structural-sink explanation. One pair, clip c030, shows mismatched\n1935\tminus original = −0.04. We retain it for transparency; the sign-test conclusion is unchanged.\n1936\t\n1937\tWe verify that null-content audio streams produce valid encoder outputs: token counts are unchanged\n1938\tacross conditions, output norms remain within a normal range, and no NaNs or extreme values occur.\n1939\tThe intervention therefore changes audio content rather than breaking the input pipeline.\n1940\t\n1941\tC.7 Video-side compression and cross-modal balance\n1942\t\n1943\tThe main paper uses within-video merging and dropping as the empirical instance of the broader\n1944\tclaim that single-modality saliency does not explicitly control cross-modal allocation. This diagnostic\n1945\tisolates video-side token selection from retained video capacity.\n1946\t\n1947\tFor each keep ratio, we compare ToMe-style bipartite cosine merging on video tokens against random\n1948\tvideo dropping. Both methods operate inside the video modality only and use the same retained\n1949\tvideo budget. After compression, we measure the resulting audio-to-video attention mass ratio in\n1950\tthe downstream LLM layers. Figure 7 shows the matched-budget comparison between ToMe-style\n1951\tmerging and random video dropping.\n1952\t\n1953\t20\n1954\t\n1955\t\fFigure 7: Within-video selector invariance at matched video budgets. ToMe-style merging and\n1956\trandom video dropping are compared at the same retained video ratios across natural-audio and\n1957\tnull-content variants. (a) The audio share trajectories nearly overlap for ToMe and random dropping.\n1958\t(b) Paired differences remain within a narrow band, showing that the dominant cross-modal effect is\n1959\tretained video capacity rather than the local video selector used inside a fixed budget.\n1960\t\n1961\tThis diagnostic isolates whether a content-aware within-video selector changes cross-modal mass\n1962\tmore than a content-blind selector when the retained video budget is fixed. Similar trajectories\n1963\tindicate that the dominant cross-modal effect is retained video capacity rather than local video-token\n1964\tidentity. The claim is not that within-video selection is irrelevant to accuracy, but that it is not an\n1965\texplicit lever for audio–video allocation.\n1966\t\n1967\tCompressing video can increase audio’s relative mass share under the shared normalizer. This effect\n1968\tdoes not mean the query has become more audio-relevant. Rather, the retained evidence composition\n1969\thas changed while the shared softmax still allocates probability mass over the remaining token set.\n1970\tThis motivates explicit capacity allocation: MACER decides how many audio and video tokens are\n1971\tretained before applying within-modality ranking.\n1972\t\n1973\tC.8 Allocation removal and within-video ranking preservation\n1974\t\n1975\tWe further separate cross-modal allocation from within-video ranking by masking audio at the query\n1976\twhile measuring both video mass and video-token ranking stability. If the audio prior were simply\n1977\tpreventing the model from finding video tokens, removing audio should substantially increase video\n1978\tmass and change the video top-K. Figure 8 shows a different pattern.\n1979\t\n1980\tThis stress test is diagnostic rather than a proposed compression method. It shows that preserving\n1981\tor improving the ranking of video tokens is not enough when the retained video budget itself is\n1982\tunderspecified. A compressor must therefore decide how many slots video receives before asking\n1983\twhich video tokens should fill those slots.\n1984\t\n1985\tC.9 Saliency replacement alone does not control global Top-K allocation\n1986\t\n1987\tEncoder-side guide methods use one modality, or a structure derived from one modality, to decide\n1988\tretention in another modality or in shared temporal chunks. This design can improve local token\n1989\tchoice when the guide modality is well aligned with answer evidence, but the guide remains a\n1990\tproxy for local salience rather than a direct control over decoder-side audio–video capacity. By\n1991\tEq. (17), audio–video attention mass contains an allocation component q⊤(ca − cv) in addition to\n1992\twithin-modality residual structure. Changing the guide direction changes which modality supplies\n1993\tthe proxy; it does not, by itself, create a controlled allocation variable. MACER therefore delays\n1994\tcompression until shallow query-conditioned decoder evidence is available, assigns audio and video\n1995\tcapacity explicitly, and then performs token ranking only within each modality.\n1996\t\n1997\t21\n1998\t\n1999\t1.000.750.500.25video keep-ratio ρ0.10.20.30.40.50.60.70.8audio share ra=ma/(ma+mv)(a)ToMe (solid) vs random drop (dashed)natural audiomismatched audiowhite noisesilence0.750.50.25video keep-ratio ρ−2−1012Δra=rToMea−rranda (pp)(b)paired gap (band: ±2 pp)\fFigure 8: Allocation changes while within-video ranking largely survives. (a) Masking audio at\n2000\tthe query produces only a small increase in video attention mass. (b) Most freed audio mass flows\n2001\tto text rather than to video, indicating that removing the audio prior does not automatically restore\n2002\tvideo capacity. (c) Within-video top-K rankings remain highly stable under the audio mask. The\n2003\tdiagnostic supports the separation used by MACER: cross-modal capacity allocation and within-\n2004\tmodality ranking are distinct decisions.\n2005\t\n2006\tD Calibration and Ablations\n2007\t\n2008\tD.1 Readout-layer sweep\n2009\t\n2010\tThis section reports the probe-layer analysis behind MACER’s asymmetric readout choice. The\n2011\tcentered sweep and heatmaps below are mechanism diagnostics for the shallow readout behavior\n2012\temphasized in the main paper. They support the 7B default (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), while the 3B setting is\n2013\tcalibrated separately and reported in Table 7.\n2014\t\n2015\tFor modality m ∈ {a, v}, define the layer-induced accuracy range\n2016\t\n2017\t∆m = max\n2018\t\n2019\tℓm∈{1,...,5}\n2020\t\n2021\tAcc(ℓm) − min\n2022\t\n2023\tℓm∈{1,...,5}\n2024\t\n2025\tAcc(ℓm),\n2026\t\n2027\t(19)\n2028\t\n2029\twhere the other modality’s probe layer is fixed to MACER’s 7B default. The ratio ∆v/∆a measures\n2030\twhether video or audio readout is more sensitive to probe-layer choice.\n2031\t\n2032\tThe key conclusion is asymmetric sensitivity, not a sharp audio optimum. Video readout changes\n2033\tdownstream accuracy substantially as the probe layer moves, whereas audio readout is comparatively\n2034\tflat across the shallow stack. MACER therefore uses an early stable audio probe and a later video\n2035\tprobe, with exact layer choices calibrated per model scale. Figure 9 summarizes the centered readout\n2036\tsweep used to diagnose audio–video readout asymmetry.\n2037\t\n2038\tFigure 9: Readout-layer asymmetry between audio and video. We sweep the probe layer of\n2039\tone modality while fixing the other modality to MACER’s 7B default readout layer, and report\n2040\ttask-centered accuracy (Acc − meanℓAcc) in percentage points. (a) The sensitivity ratio ∆v/∆a is\n2041\tabove one in most benchmark–retention settings, showing that video readout is more layer-sensitive\n2042\tthan audio readout. (b) Sweeping the video probe layer yields a consistent preference for later\n2043\tshallow layers, especially L4–L5. (c) Sweeping the audio probe layer produces small and inconsistent\n2044\tfluctuations around zero. These results motivate MACER’s asymmetric readout: an early stable audio\n2045\tprobe and a later video probe.\n2046\t\n2047\t22\n2048\t\n2049\t\fD.2 Full 5x5 readout heatmaps\n2050\t\n2051\tFigure 10 expands the centered readout sweep to all 5 × 5 audio–video probe-layer pairs under the\n2052\tcalibrated 7B capacity split.\n2053\t\n2054\tFigure 10: Full 5 × 5 audio-video probe-layer sweep at the 7B calibrated retained-audio share\n2055\tα = 0.30 and representative keep ratio ρ = 0.25. Rows vary the audio probe layer and columns\n2056\tvary the video probe layer under the same compression budget. Heatmaps use task-local color\n2057\tscales; numbers are raw scores. The fixed-share setting aligns the supplementary heatmap with\n2058\tthe main-paper MACER configuration and supports the same asymmetry: video readout is more\n2059\tlayer-sensitive than audio readout in most tasks.\n2060\t\n2061\tD.3 Asymmetry ratio across retention regimes\n2062\t\n2063\tVideo sensitivity is strongest at the representative operating point ρ = 0.25. At the more aggressive\n2064\tρ = 0.10, the asymmetry weakens and can reverse on AVUTBench. MACER’s default readout\n2065\tremains fixed across benchmarks and keep ratios within each model scale; the reversal mainly\n2066\texplains why extremely tight retention is less stable. The video-side sweep is consistent with the\n2067\tmechanism analysis: video tokens become more useful for within-modality ranking in the L4–L5\n2068\tband, whereas earlier layers are more allocation-dominated. The audio-side sweep does not show a\n2069\tsharp global optimum; it shows that shallow audio readout is broadly stable. Table 13 reports the\n2070\tcorresponding sensitivity ratios across retention regimes.\n2071\t\n2072\tTable 13: Readout sensitivity ratio under two retention regimes. ∆m is the accuracy range induced\n2073\tby sweeping modality m’s probe layer. Video readout is more sensitive than audio readout in most\n2074\tsettings, especially at ρ = 0.25. At ρ = 0.10, the asymmetry weakens and reverses on AVUTBench,\n2075\tindicating that extremely tight budgets can change the relative importance of probe-layer choice.\n2076\t\n2077\tBenchmark\n2078\t\n2079\t∆v/∆a at ρ = 0.25 ∆v/∆a at ρ = 0.10\n2080\t\n2081\tAVUTBench\n2082\tDailyOmni\n2083\tVideo-MME\n2084\tWorldSense\n2085\t\n2086\t8.91×\n2087\t1.40×\n2088\t12.50×\n2089\t27.00×\n2090\t\n2091\t0.76×\n2092\t2.40×\n2093\t4.29×\n2094\t9.00×\n2095\t\n2096\tD.4 Retained-audio share robustness\n2097\t\n2098\tTable 14 reports a held-out robustness audit of the retained-audio share at the representative aggressive\n2099\tsetting ρ = 0.25. The sweep uses the full MACER procedure with the 7B readout and coverage\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "880fc986-7020-4816-81bd-00fc9d73bafb", "uuid": "72761619-ff05-49a4-85ed-93bde555eb02", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:33:58.651Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\nwhere cm is the per-clip modality centroid and ϵ(m)\nis the within-modality residual. The usual\nattention scaling factor is absorbed into q. For audio–video comparison, the shared softmax normalizer\ncancels:\n\ni\n\nlog\n\nmassa(q)\nmassv(q)\n\n\n\n\n\n= q⊤(ca − cv) +\n\nlog\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(a)\n\ni\n\n) − log\n\n(cid:88)\n\ni∈a\n\n(cid:88)\n\nj∈v\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(v)\nj )\n\n .\n\n(17)\n\nThe first term is the cross-modal allocation term; the bracketed term is the difference between within-\nmodality residual aggregation terms. The bracketed term is not a pure token-relevance term because\nit also contains token-count and residual-concentration effects. The main point is that raw shared\nattention necessarily mixes a cross-modal mass term with modality-local variation, while a shared\ntop-K rule uses the mixed quantity for token retention.\n\nThe residual aggregation term contains a token-count component:\n\nlog\n\n(cid:88)\n\ni∈m\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(m)\n\ni\n\n) = log nm + log\n\n(cid:32)\n\n1\nnm\n\n(cid:88)\n\ni∈m\n\n(cid:33)\n\nexp(q⊤ϵ(m)\n\ni\n\n)\n\n.\n\n(18)\n\nThis separates token count from residual concentration. Under the default 12-frame setting, video\ncontributes nv = 864 tokens, while audio has median na ≈ 1460. Therefore, the audio-favoring\nattention pattern is not a consequence of video having more raw tokens. Conversely, MACER’s\nretained capacity split deliberately under-samples audio relative to its raw token share to preserve\nsufficient video evidence under aggressive pruning.\n\nC.2 Q-centroid mechanism\n\nEquation (17) identifies q⊤(ca − cv) as the cross-modal allocation term. We therefore test whether\nthe observed audio prior is mainly driven by query-side alignment with the modality centroids, rather\nthan by key-side geometry alone or by within-audio coherence. Figure 5 reports three complementary\ndiagnostics: interventions on the query direction, layer-wise query-centroid alignment, and the\npersistence of the same bias at the terminal decode position.\n\n17\n\n\fFigure 5: Q-centroid mechanism behind the audio prior. (a) Query-side interventions show\nthat audio mass is strongly controlled by alignment between the query direction and the modality\ncentroids; key-side geometry and within-audio coherence controls alone do not explain the prior. (b)\nAcross layers, the query representation is directionally closer to the audio centroid than to the video\ncentroid. (c) The same Q-side audio bias is visible at the decode terminal position, indicating that the\nallocation tendency is not limited to the averaged query-token span.\n\nThis diagnostic is consistent with the decomposition: the observed behavior is not merely that audio\nkeys are numerous or locally coherent, but that the shared decoder places query directions in an audio-\nfavoring centroid geometry. This is why raw shared attention can remain a useful content-responsive\nsignal while still being a poor cross-modal capacity allocator.\n\nC.3 Packing structure and distance-matched controls\n\nTo rule out a simple prefix-sink explanation, we inspect the input packing order. The observed\nstructure is\n\n[system/user/template] → ⟨vision_bos⟩ ⟨audio_bos⟩ → V288 → A50 → V288 → A50 →\nV288 → Arest → [query]\n\nAudio is interleaved with video blocks rather than placed as a pure prefix. The largest audio block\nappears close to the query, motivating the distance-matched control below.\n\nWe control for token distance to the query using quantile-binned non-parametric analysis. Table 9\nreports the equal-distance audio–video ratio at selected layers. Early layers show that distance\nexplains a large part of the apparent audio advantage; mid-to-late layers retain an audio-favoring\nresidual even after distance matching.\n\nTable 9: Distance-matched analysis. At early layers, matching token distance largely removes the\naudio advantage. Starting in the mid stack, a residual audio advantage persists after controlling for\ndistance, indicating that the effect is not only a recency artifact.\n\nLayer\n\nEqual-distance log(v/a)\n\nv/a ratio\n\nInterpretation\n\nL7\nL10\nL14\nL19\n\n−0.012\n+0.007\n−0.725\n−0.886\n\n0.988\n1.007\n0.484\n0.412\n\nPositional bias dominates\nEqual-distance parity\nAudio residual emerges\nAudio residual remains stable\n\nDistance matching and null-content ablation probe different confounds: distance matching controls\ntoken position while keeping original content, whereas mismatched-audio control disrupts semantic\naudio–video coupling while keeping a real audio stream. At L14, the two estimates are numerically\nclose: distance-matched analysis gives log(v/a) = −0.725, while the mismatched-audio control\ngives log(v/a) = −0.746. The close agreement between the distance-matched and mismatched-\naudio estimates supports the same qualitative conclusion: the mid-layer residual is not explained by\neither position or semantic alignment alone.\n\nWe also fit log-linear regressions\n\nlog(attention) = β log(distance) + ϵ\n\n18\n\n\fper layer. The R2 values range from 0.000 to 0.036, indicating that attention–distance relationships\nare not well described by a simple power law. The quantile-binned non-parametric analysis above is\nused for this reason and does not rely on the regression model being well specified.\n\nC.4 Per-layer attention statistics\n\nTable 10 reports per-token attention statistics across all 28 decoder layers on the paired-query\ndiagnostic subset (30 clips × 2 query types × 28 layers, head-aggregated, 1680 cells total).\n\nTable 10: Per-layer audio–video attention statistics on selected layers. Video wins the per-token\nattention comparison in only 8.8% of layer–clip–query cells pooled across query types and layers.\nv > a rate\n\nlog(v/a) median\n\nInterpretation\n\nLayer\n\nL0\nL3\nL7\nL9\nL10\nL14\nL19\nL27\n\nPooled\n\n−3.02\n−2.14\n−0.73\n−0.31\n−0.42\n−0.89\n−1.03\n−0.96\n\n−0.96\n\n0/60\n0/60\n10/60\n13/60\n12/60\n7/60\n9/60\n9/60\n\nStrong early positional bias\nStrong early positional bias\nPositional effect decaying\nWeakest audio bias\nTransition boundary\nAudio residual emerging\nAudio residual established\nFinal layer\n\n148/1680 (8.8%)\n\nUnder video-essential queries, video wins in 12.1% of cells (102/840). Under audio-essential queries,\nvideo wins in 5.5% of cells (46/840). The query shifts the ratio systematically in the predicted\ndirection, but does not reliably remove the baseline preference for audio. This supports the main-\npaper phrasing “query-robust” rather than the stronger and less precise “query-invariant.”\n\nC.5 Frame-count diagnostic\n\nTo check whether the 12-frame default creates the audio advantage, we vary the video frame count\non three paired clips. Table 11 shows that the v/a per-token ratio monotonically decreases with\nmore video tokens in this small diagnostic, suggesting that the 12-frame default is not unusually\nunfavorable to video in this diagnostic.\n\nTable 11: Frame-count diagnostic. The v/a ratio decreases monotonically as frame count increases\non the three tested paired clips, indicating that the 12-frame default is not obviously unfavorable to\nvideo among the tested settings. Higher frame counts were OOM on our hardware.\n\nFrames\n\nnv\n\nv/a ratio Monotonic pairs\n\nSign flips\n\n12\n16\n20\n\n864\n1152\n1440\n\n0.215\n0.200\n0.179\n\n–\n3/3 vs. 12\n3/3 vs. 16\n\n–\n0\n0\n\nC.6 Null-content controls\n\nWe replace original audio with silence, scale-matched white noise, or mismatched real audio. Silence\nand white noise nearly remove the audio advantage, while mismatched audio produces an intermediate\nshift. This graded response shows that audio attention is not an input-independent sink: the shared\nattention space is responsive to audio content. Content responsiveness, however, does not make\nraw attention magnitude a calibrated capacity signal under compression. Table 12 reports the\ncorresponding shifts in mid-layer log(v/a) relative to the original-audio condition, and Figure 6\nvisualizes the same null-content and distance-matched controls.\n\n19\n\n\fTable 12: Shift in mid-layer log(v/a) relative to original audio across 30 paired clips. Silence and\nwhite noise produce large positive shifts, while mismatched audio produces a smaller but still positive\nshift.\n\nCondition\n\nMean shift Median shift\n\nMin\n\nMax\n\nSilence\nWhite noise\nMismatched\n\n+1.83\n+1.60\n+0.52\n\n+1.80\n+1.55\n+0.48\n\n+1.17 +2.81\n+0.81 +2.49\n−0.04 +1.66\n\nFigure 6: Null-content and distance-matched controls for the audio prior. (a) Replacing original\naudio with mismatched real audio, scale-matched white noise, or silence shifts log(v/a) toward\nvideo; the prior therefore responds to audio content rather than behaving as an input-independent\nsink. (b) Equal-distance matching removes much of the early positional effect, but a mid-layer\naudio-favoring residual remains. (c) At L14, the mismatched-audio and distance-matched controls\ngive nearly the same residual estimate, supporting the conclusion that neither recency nor semantic\naudio–video alignment alone explains the mid-layer effect.\n\nSign tests against the null hypothesis of zero shift give silence (30/30 pairs, p = 9.3 × 10−10), white\nnoise (30/30, p = 9.3 × 10−10), and mismatched audio (29/30, p = 2.9 × 10−8). These tests support\nthe direction of the shift, and the tabled means and medians summarize the paired intervention subset.\n\nThe original-to-mismatched shift (+0.52) reflects loss of semantic video–audio alignment while\npreserving acoustic structure. The mismatched-to-white-noise shift (+1.08) reflects destruction of\nsemantic audio content. The white-noise-to-silence shift (+0.23) reflects the residual contribution\nof acoustic energy versus complete absence. The graded collapse supports a content-conditioned\ninterpretation rather than a pure structural-sink explanation. One pair, clip c030, shows mismatched\nminus original = −0.04. We retain it for transparency; the sign-test conclusion is unchanged.\n\nWe verify that null-content audio streams produce valid encoder outputs: token counts are unchanged\nacross conditions, output norms remain within a normal range, and no NaNs or extreme values occur.\nThe intervention therefore changes audio content rather than breaking the input pipeline.\n\nC.7 Video-side compression and cross-modal balance\n\nThe main paper uses within-video merging and dropping as the empirical instance of the broader\nclaim that single-modality saliency does not explicitly control cross-modal allocation. This diagnostic\nisolates video-side token selection from retained video capacity.\n\nFor each keep ratio, we compare ToMe-style bipartite cosine merging on video tokens against random\nvideo dropping. Both methods operate inside the video modality only and use the same retained\nvideo budget. After compression, we measure the resulting audio-to-video attention mass ratio in\nthe downstream LLM layers. Figure 7 shows the matched-budget comparison between ToMe-style\nmerging and random video dropping.\n\n20\n\n\fFigure 7: Within-video selector invariance at matched video budgets. ToMe-style merging and\nrandom video dropping are compared at the same retained video ratios across natural-audio and\nnull-content variants. (a) The audio share trajectories nearly overlap for ToMe and random dropping.\n(b) Paired differences remain within a narrow band, showing that the dominant cross-modal effect is\nretained video capacity rather than the local video selector used inside a fixed budget.\n\nThis diagnostic isolates whether a content-aware within-video selector changes cross-modal mass\nmore than a content-blind selector when the retained video budget is fixed. Similar trajectories\nindicate that the dominant cross-modal effect is retained video capacity rather than local video-token\nidentity. The claim is not that within-video selection is irrelevant to accuracy, but that it is not an\nexplicit lever for audio–video allocation.\n\nCompressing video can increase audio’s relative mass share under the shared normalizer. This effect\ndoes not mean the query has become more audio-relevant. Rather, the retained evidence composition\nhas changed while the shared softmax still allocates probability mass over the remaining token set.\nThis motivates explicit capacity allocation: MACER decides how many audio and video tokens are\nretained before applying within-modality ranking.\n\nC.8 Allocation removal and within-video ranking preservation\n\nWe further separate cross-modal allocation from within-video ranking by masking audio at the query\nwhile measuring both video mass and video-token ranking stability. If the audio prior were simply\npreventing the model from finding video tokens, removing audio should substantially increase video\nmass and change the video top-K. Figure 8 shows a different pattern.\n\nThis stress test is diagnostic rather than a proposed compression method. It shows that preserving\nor improving the ranking of video tokens is not enough when the retained video budget itself is\nunderspecified. A compressor must therefore decide how many slots video receives before asking\nwhich video tokens should fill those slots.\n\nC.9 Saliency replacement alone does not control global Top-K allocation\n\nEncoder-side guide methods use one modality, or a structure derived from one modality, to decide\nretention in another modality or in shared temporal chunks. This design can improve local token\nchoice when the guide modality is well aligned with answer evidence, but the guide remains a\nproxy for local salience rather than a direct control over decoder-side audio–video capacity. By\nEq. (17), audio–video attention mass contains an allocation component q⊤(ca − cv) in addition to\nwithin-modality residual structure. Changing the guide direction changes which modality supplies\nthe proxy; it does not, by itself, create a controlled allocation variable. MACER therefore delays\ncompression until shallow query-conditioned decoder evidence is available, assigns audio and video\ncapacity explicitly, and then performs token ranking only within each modality.\n\n21\n\n1.000.750.500.25video keep-ratio ρ0.10.20.30.40.50.60.70.8audio share ra=ma/(ma+mv)(a)ToMe (solid) vs random drop (dashed)natural audiomismatched audiowhite noisesilence0.750.50.25video keep-ratio ρ−2−1012Δra=rToMea−rranda (pp)(b)paired gap (band: ±2 pp)\fFigure 8: Allocation changes while within-video ranking largely survives. (a) Masking audio at\nthe query produces only a small increase in video attention mass. (b) Most freed audio mass flows\nto text rather than to video, indicating that removing the audio prior does not automatically restore\nvideo capacity. (c) Within-video top-K rankings remain highly stable under the audio mask. The\ndiagnostic supports the separation used by MACER: cross-modal capacity allocation and within-\nmodality ranking are distinct decisions.\n\nD Calibration and Ablations\n\nD.1 Readout-layer sweep\n\nThis section reports the probe-layer analysis behind MACER’s asymmetric readout choice. The\ncentered sweep and heatmaps below are mechanism diagnostics for the shallow readout behavior\nemphasized in the main paper. They support the 7B default (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), while the 3B setting is\ncalibrated separately and reported in Table 7.\n\nFor modality m ∈ {a, v}, define the layer-induced accuracy range\n\n∆m = max\n\nℓm∈{1,...,5}\n\nAcc(ℓm) − min\n\nℓm∈{1,...,5}\n\nAcc(ℓm),\n\n(19)\n\nwhere the other modality’s probe layer is fixed to MACER’s 7B default. The ratio ∆v/∆a measures\nwhether video or audio readout is more sensitive to probe-layer choice.\n\nThe key conclusion is asymmetric sensitivity, not a sharp audio optimum. Video readout changes\ndownstream accuracy substantially as the probe layer moves, whereas audio readout is comparatively\nflat across the shallow stack. MACER therefore uses an early stable audio probe and a later video\nprobe, with exact layer choices calibrated per model scale. Figure 9 summarizes the centered readout\nsweep used to diagnose audio–video readout asymmetry.\n\nFigure 9: Readout-layer asymmetry between audio and video. We sweep the probe layer of\none modality while fixing the other modality to MACER’s 7B default readout layer, and report\ntask-centered accuracy (Acc − meanℓAcc) in percentage points. (a) The sensitivity ratio ∆v/∆a is\nabove one in most benchmark–retention settings, showing that video readout is more layer-sensitive\nthan audio readout. (b) Sweeping the video probe layer yields a consistent preference for later\nshallow layers, especially L4–L5. (c) Sweeping the audio probe layer produces small and inconsistent\nfluctuations around zero. These results motivate MACER’s asymmetric readout: an early stable audio\nprobe and a later video probe.\n\n22\n\n\fD.2 Full 5x5 readout heatmaps\n\nFigure 10 expands the centered readout sweep to all 5 × 5 audio–video probe-layer pairs under the\ncalibrated 7B capacity split.\n\nFigure 10: Full 5 × 5 audio-video probe-layer sweep at the 7B calibrated retained-audio share\nα = 0.30 and representative keep ratio ρ = 0.25. Rows vary the audio probe layer and columns\nvary the video probe layer under the same compression budget. Heatmaps use task-local color\nscales; numbers are raw scores. The fixed-share setting aligns the supplementary heatmap with\nthe main-paper MACER configuration and supports the same asymmetry: video readout is more\nlayer-sensitive than audio readout in most tasks.\n\nD.3 Asymmetry ratio across retention regimes\n\nVideo sensitivity is strongest at the representative operating point ρ = 0.25. At the more aggressive\nρ = 0.10, the asymmetry weakens and can reverse on AVUTBench. MACER’s default readout\nremains fixed across benchmarks and keep ratios within each model scale; the reversal mainly\nexplains why extremely tight retention is less stable. The video-side sweep is consistent with the\nmechanism analysis: video tokens become more useful for within-modality ranking in the L4–L5\nband, whereas earlier layers are more allocation-dominated. The audio-side sweep does not show a\nsharp global optimum; it shows that shallow audio readout is broadly stable. Table 13 reports the\ncorresponding sensitivity ratios across retention regimes.\n\nTable 13: Readout sensitivity ratio under two retention regimes. ∆m is the accuracy range induced\nby sweeping modality m’s probe layer. Video readout is more sensitive than audio readout in most\nsettings, especially at ρ = 0.25. At ρ = 0.10, the asymmetry weakens and reverses on AVUTBench,\nindicating that extremely tight budgets can change the relative importance of probe-layer choice.\n\nBenchmark\n\n∆v/∆a at ρ = 0.25 ∆v/∆a at ρ = 0.10\n\nAVUTBench\nDailyOmni\nVideo-MME\nWorldSense\n\n8.91×\n1.40×\n12.50×\n27.00×\n\n0.76×\n2.40×\n4.29×\n9.00×\n\nD.4 Retained-audio share robustness\n\nTable 14 reports a held-out robustness audit of the retained-audio share at the representative aggressive\nsetting ρ = 0.25. The sweep uses the full MACER procedure with the 7B readout and coverage", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1600, "totalLines": 2604}}}
