{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8100ed425880529d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2100\tdefaults, (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5) and λc = 0.20, and varies only α. This audit is used to check whether\n2101\tthe fixed retained-audio share is brittle; it is not used to select benchmark-specific or query-specific\n2102\thyperparameters.\n2103\t\n2104\tThe deployed 7B setting α = 0.30 achieves the best normalized average among fixed settings. The\n2105\tneighboring setting α = 0.40 is only 0.11 normalized points lower, and a diagnostic oracle that selects\n2106\tα separately for each benchmark is only 0.92 points higher. This supports interpreting α = 0.30 as a\n2107\tstable operating-band choice rather than a fragile benchmark-specific setting.\n2108\t\n2109\t23\n2110\t\n2111\t\fAt the default 12-frame setting, audio contributes more raw tokens than video (median na ≈ 1460,\n2112\tnv = 864). Thus α = 0.30 is not a token-count-matching choice. It deliberately retains fewer audio\n2113\ttokens than audio’s raw share, counterbalancing the audio-favoring allocation prior while preserving\n2114\tenough audio capacity for audio-grounded and audio–video joint tasks.\n2115\t\n2116\tTable 14: Robustness of the retained-audio share α on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. We sweep α at\n2117\tρ = 25% with full MACER, (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), and λc = 0.20 on a 200-example held-out robustness\n2118\tsplit spanning all four benchmark families. Norm. Avg. is computed by normalizing each row to\n2119\tthe Full tokens row on the same split and then averaging across the four benchmark families. The\n2120\tdeployed setting α = 0.30 achieves the best normalized average among fixed settings; the neighboring\n2121\tsetting α = 0.40 is only 0.11 points lower, and a diagnostic benchmark-level oracle improves by only\n2122\t0.92 points. Thus coarse task- or benchmark-conditioned allocation has limited headroom over the\n2123\tfrozen deployment setting, supporting the interpretation of α as a stable operating-band choice rather\n2124\tthan a brittle benchmark-specific hyperparameter.\n2125\t\n2126\tSetting\n2127\t\n2128\tFull tokens\n2129\t\n2130\tα = 0.10\n2131\tα = 0.20\n2132\tα = 0.30 (frozen)\n2133\tα = 0.40\n2134\tα = 0.50\n2135\t\n2136\tAVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Video-MME Norm. Avg.\n2137\t\n2138\t65.83\n2139\t\n2140\t63.32\n2141\t64.82\n2142\t66.50\n2143\t67.00\n2144\t64.00\n2145\t\n2146\t69.50\n2147\t\n2148\t65.00\n2149\t67.00\n2150\t68.50\n2151\t68.00\n2152\t66.50\n2153\t\n2154\t68.50\n2155\t\n2156\t44.00\n2157\t\n2158\t44.50\n2159\t44.00\n2160\t43.50\n2161\t43.00\n2162\t42.50\n2163\t\n2164\t44.50\n2165\t\n2166\t77.00\n2167\t\n2168\t74.00\n2169\t74.50\n2170\t74.50\n2171\t75.00\n2172\t74.00\n2173\t\n2174\t75.00\n2175\t\n2176\t100.00\n2177\t\n2178\t96.74\n2179\t97.91\n2180\t98.80\n2181\t98.69\n2182\t96.40\n2183\t\n2184\t99.72\n2185\t\n2186\tOracle per-benchmark α (diagnostic)\n2187\t\n2188\t67.00\n2189\t\n2190\tD.5 Coverage concentration and ablation\n2191\t\n2192\tWe bin retained tokens into native 2-second temporal blocks and measure temporal concentration\n2193\twith the Gini coefficient. Lower Gini indicates more uniform timeline coverage. Table 15 reports the\n2194\ttemporal concentration diagnostic that motivates the video-local coverage term.\n2195\t\n2196\tThe no-coverage ablation sets λc = 0, which recovers pure video top-K under the same video\n2197\tbudget Kv, the same modality-local video scores s(v), and the same tie-breaking rule. Audio\n2198\tselection is unchanged and remains modality-local top-K. Thus the ablation isolates the video-side\n2199\tchunk-coverage term from the core allocation–selection separation.\n2200\t\n2201\tTable 15: Temporal concentration of retained tokens under score-only selection. Video selection is\n2202\tmore temporally concentrated than audio selection, motivating a video-specific score-aware chunk-\n2203\tcoverage greedy rule.\n2204\t\n2205\tRetention Audio Gini Video Gini\n2206\t\n2207\tInterpretation\n2208\t\n2209\tρ = 0.25\n2210\t\n2211\t0.06\n2212\t\n2213\t0.33\n2214\t\n2215\tVideo is substantially more concentrated\n2216\t\n2217\tMain Table 4 reports the full component ablation. We keep the temporal-concentration diagnostic\n2218\there to explain why the coverage term is included, but we do not duplicate the ablation table in the\n2219\tappendix.\n2220\t\n2221\tD.6 Matched-retention comparison with DASH\n2222\t\n2223\tTable 16 provides a matched-retention comparison with the concurrent DASH compressor. The\n2224\tcomparison is included as an appendix baseline check because it covers a subset of the full main-table\n2225\tgrid. MACER improves over DASH on five of six reported cells and is effectively tied on WorldSense\n2226\tat 25% retention.\n2227\t\n2228\t24\n2229\t\n2230\t\fTable 16: Comparison with DASH [Li and Huang, 2026] under matched retention. MACER improves\n2231\tover DASH on five of six cells and is effectively tied on WorldSense at 25% retention.\n2232\tRetain\n2233\t\n2234\tDASH MACER\n2235\t\n2236\tBenchmark\n2237\t\n2238\t∆\n2239\t\n2240\tAVUTBench\n2241\tAVUTBench\n2242\tWorldSense\n2243\tDailyOmni\n2244\tDailyOmni\n2245\tDailyOmni\n2246\t\n2247\t25%\n2248\t35%\n2249\t25%\n2250\t25%\n2251\t35%\n2252\t45%\n2253\t\n2254\t60.90\n2255\t61.50\n2256\t44.90\n2257\t59.32\n2258\t60.65\n2259\t61.49\n2260\t\n2261\t63.67\n2262\t64.63\n2263\t44.80\n2264\t62.07\n2265\t62.32\n2266\t63.32\n2267\t\n2268\t+2.77\n2269\t+3.13\n2270\t-0.10\n2271\t+2.75\n2272\t+1.67\n2273\t+1.83\n2274\t\n2275\tE Inference Cost Reporting\n2276\t\n2277\tMACER prunes after shallow decoder-side readouts. For a conservative estimate, we charge full\n2278\tmultimodal computation through the later modality readout layer, and charge the remaining decoder\n2279\tlayers at the retained multimodal sequence length. This convention does not claim additional credit\n2280\tfor the earlier audio-pruning stage.\n2281\t\n2282\tE.1 Token-layer cost proxy\n2283\t\n2284\tIgnoring text tokens and constant factors, the normalized multimodal token-layer cost is\n2285\t\n2286\t(na + nv)Lp + Kmm(L − Lp)\n2287\t(na + nv)L\n2288\t\n2289\t≈\n2290\t\n2291\tLp + ρ(L − Lp)\n2292\tL\n2293\t\n2294\t.\n2295\t\n2296\t(20)\n2297\t\n2298\tTable 17 reports the resulting proxy values for the scale-specific pruning depths used in the main\n2299\tpaper.\n2300\t\n2301\tTable 17: Approximate multimodal token-layer cost under scale-specific pruning depths. These\n2302\tvalues are a cost proxy rather than wall-clock latency.\n2303\tKeep ratio ρ\n2304\t\n2305\t3B: L = 36, Lp = 7\n2306\t\n2307\t7B: L = 28, Lp = 5\n2308\t\n2309\t0.10\n2310\t0.25\n2311\t0.35\n2312\t0.45\n2313\t\n2314\t26.1%\n2315\t38.4%\n2316\t46.6%\n2317\t54.8%\n2318\t\n2319\t27.5%\n2320\t39.6%\n2321\t47.6%\n2322\t55.7%\n2323\t\n2324\tE.2 FLOPs ratios used in the main tables\n2325\t\n2326\tThe main comparison table uses attention-corrected FLOPs accounting matched to the baseline\n2327\treporting protocol. Let η denote the attention share of the decoder computation. Input-prune methods\n2328\tuse\n2329\t\n2330\twhile probe-prune methods with a shallow full-context readout proxy use\n2331\t\n2332\tρ(1 − η(1 − ρ)),\n2333\t\n2334\tLp + (L − Lp)ρ(1 − η(1 − ρ))\n2335\tL\n2336\t\n2337\t.\n2338\t\n2339\t(21)\n2340\t\n2341\t(22)\n2342\t\n2343\tFor MACER, we set Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv) when reporting estimated FLOPs. This is a conservative\n2344\taccounting proxy for the staged implementation because it charges all multimodal tokens until the\n2345\tlater video readout rather than crediting the earlier audio-pruning stage. For Qwen2.5-Omni-7B we\n2346\tuse L = 28, Lp = 5, and η = 0.235. For Qwen2.5-Omni-3B we use L = 36, Lp = 7, and η = 0.36,\n2347\tmatching the scale-specific readout settings in Table 7. Table 18 gives the corresponding MACER\n2348\tFLOPs ratios.\n2349\t\n2350\t25\n2351\t\n2352\t\fTable 18: Attention-corrected MACER FLOPs ratios computed from Eq. (22). The values are matched\n2353\tto the revised main-table FLOPs accounting.\n2354\t\n2355\tKeep ratio ρ\n2356\t\n2357\t7B MACER FLOPs ratio\n2358\t\n2359\t3B MACER FLOPs ratio\n2360\t\n2361\t0.45\n2362\t0.35\n2363\t0.25\n2364\t\n2365\t50.1%\n2366\t42.2%\n2367\t34.8%\n2368\t\n2369\t48.5%\n2370\t41.0%\n2371\t34.1%\n2372\t\n2373\tE.3 Measured latency table moved to the main paper\n2374\t\n2375\tMain Table 3 reports the WorldSense latency and memory measurement. We keep the FLOPs\n2376\taccounting in this appendix because it defines the architecture-level cost estimate, but the measured\n2377\twall-clock evidence now appears in the main paper so reviewers can inspect it without consulting the\n2378\tappendix.\n2379\t\n2380\tF Cross-Backbone Transfer Study\n2381\t\n2382\tAdditional backbone: OmniVinci-9B. To test whether MACER’s allocation–selection design is\n2383\tspecific to Qwen2.5-Omni, we further evaluate it on OmniVinci-9B [Ye et al., 2025a], an NVLabs\n2384\tomnimodal backbone. We keep the shallow readout and one-shot pruning schedule identical to the\n2385\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B MACER configuration: audio scores are read at L3, video scores at L5. Thus, the\n2386\ttransfer study changes the backbone and sweeps only the retained-audio share α, while keeping the\n2387\tvideo coverage strength fixed at λc = 0.20. Table 19 reports the OmniVinci-9B transfer results.\n2388\t\n2389\tTable 19: Cross-backbone transfer to OmniVinci-9B with explicit capacity split. DailyOmni and\n2390\tWorldSense use the corresponding full-token reference runs for normalization. Cons. Norm. Avg.\n2391\tnormalizes each benchmark score to the corresponding full-token result and averages across AVUT,\n2392\tDailyOmni, and WorldSense. MACER-A (α = 0.20) yields consistent gains across all three retention\n2393\tlevels, while MACER-B (α = 0.40) is strongest in the moderate-retention regime (ρ ∈ {35%, 45%}),\n2394\twith mean +7.50 pp over shared top-K. Superscripts mark the full-token reference runs used for\n2395\tnormalization.\n2396\tMethod\n2397\t\n2398\tAVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Cons. Norm. Avg. ∆ vs. Shared\n2399\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "880fc986-7020-4816-81bd-00fc9d73bafb", "uuid": "03447057-32a7-409a-b437-ac76f519f0d5", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T05:34:02.922Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "defaults, (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5) and λc = 0.20, and varies only α. This audit is used to check whether\nthe fixed retained-audio share is brittle; it is not used to select benchmark-specific or query-specific\nhyperparameters.\n\nThe deployed 7B setting α = 0.30 achieves the best normalized average among fixed settings. The\nneighboring setting α = 0.40 is only 0.11 normalized points lower, and a diagnostic oracle that selects\nα separately for each benchmark is only 0.92 points higher. This supports interpreting α = 0.30 as a\nstable operating-band choice rather than a fragile benchmark-specific setting.\n\n23\n\n\fAt the default 12-frame setting, audio contributes more raw tokens than video (median na ≈ 1460,\nnv = 864). Thus α = 0.30 is not a token-count-matching choice. It deliberately retains fewer audio\ntokens than audio’s raw share, counterbalancing the audio-favoring allocation prior while preserving\nenough audio capacity for audio-grounded and audio–video joint tasks.\n\nTable 14: Robustness of the retained-audio share α on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. We sweep α at\nρ = 25% with full MACER, (ℓa, ℓv) = (3, 5), and λc = 0.20 on a 200-example held-out robustness\nsplit spanning all four benchmark families. Norm. Avg. is computed by normalizing each row to\nthe Full tokens row on the same split and then averaging across the four benchmark families. The\ndeployed setting α = 0.30 achieves the best normalized average among fixed settings; the neighboring\nsetting α = 0.40 is only 0.11 points lower, and a diagnostic benchmark-level oracle improves by only\n0.92 points. Thus coarse task- or benchmark-conditioned allocation has limited headroom over the\nfrozen deployment setting, supporting the interpretation of α as a stable operating-band choice rather\nthan a brittle benchmark-specific hyperparameter.\n\nSetting\n\nFull tokens\n\nα = 0.10\nα = 0.20\nα = 0.30 (frozen)\nα = 0.40\nα = 0.50\n\nAVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Video-MME Norm. Avg.\n\n65.83\n\n63.32\n64.82\n66.50\n67.00\n64.00\n\n69.50\n\n65.00\n67.00\n68.50\n68.00\n66.50\n\n68.50\n\n44.00\n\n44.50\n44.00\n43.50\n43.00\n42.50\n\n44.50\n\n77.00\n\n74.00\n74.50\n74.50\n75.00\n74.00\n\n75.00\n\n100.00\n\n96.74\n97.91\n98.80\n98.69\n96.40\n\n99.72\n\nOracle per-benchmark α (diagnostic)\n\n67.00\n\nD.5 Coverage concentration and ablation\n\nWe bin retained tokens into native 2-second temporal blocks and measure temporal concentration\nwith the Gini coefficient. Lower Gini indicates more uniform timeline coverage. Table 15 reports the\ntemporal concentration diagnostic that motivates the video-local coverage term.\n\nThe no-coverage ablation sets λc = 0, which recovers pure video top-K under the same video\nbudget Kv, the same modality-local video scores s(v), and the same tie-breaking rule. Audio\nselection is unchanged and remains modality-local top-K. Thus the ablation isolates the video-side\nchunk-coverage term from the core allocation–selection separation.\n\nTable 15: Temporal concentration of retained tokens under score-only selection. Video selection is\nmore temporally concentrated than audio selection, motivating a video-specific score-aware chunk-\ncoverage greedy rule.\n\nRetention Audio Gini Video Gini\n\nInterpretation\n\nρ = 0.25\n\n0.06\n\n0.33\n\nVideo is substantially more concentrated\n\nMain Table 4 reports the full component ablation. We keep the temporal-concentration diagnostic\nhere to explain why the coverage term is included, but we do not duplicate the ablation table in the\nappendix.\n\nD.6 Matched-retention comparison with DASH\n\nTable 16 provides a matched-retention comparison with the concurrent DASH compressor. The\ncomparison is included as an appendix baseline check because it covers a subset of the full main-table\ngrid. MACER improves over DASH on five of six reported cells and is effectively tied on WorldSense\nat 25% retention.\n\n24\n\n\fTable 16: Comparison with DASH [Li and Huang, 2026] under matched retention. MACER improves\nover DASH on five of six cells and is effectively tied on WorldSense at 25% retention.\nRetain\n\nDASH MACER\n\nBenchmark\n\n∆\n\nAVUTBench\nAVUTBench\nWorldSense\nDailyOmni\nDailyOmni\nDailyOmni\n\n25%\n35%\n25%\n25%\n35%\n45%\n\n60.90\n61.50\n44.90\n59.32\n60.65\n61.49\n\n63.67\n64.63\n44.80\n62.07\n62.32\n63.32\n\n+2.77\n+3.13\n-0.10\n+2.75\n+1.67\n+1.83\n\nE Inference Cost Reporting\n\nMACER prunes after shallow decoder-side readouts. For a conservative estimate, we charge full\nmultimodal computation through the later modality readout layer, and charge the remaining decoder\nlayers at the retained multimodal sequence length. This convention does not claim additional credit\nfor the earlier audio-pruning stage.\n\nE.1 Token-layer cost proxy\n\nIgnoring text tokens and constant factors, the normalized multimodal token-layer cost is\n\n(na + nv)Lp + Kmm(L − Lp)\n(na + nv)L\n\n≈\n\nLp + ρ(L − Lp)\nL\n\n.\n\n(20)\n\nTable 17 reports the resulting proxy values for the scale-specific pruning depths used in the main\npaper.\n\nTable 17: Approximate multimodal token-layer cost under scale-specific pruning depths. These\nvalues are a cost proxy rather than wall-clock latency.\nKeep ratio ρ\n\n3B: L = 36, Lp = 7\n\n7B: L = 28, Lp = 5\n\n0.10\n0.25\n0.35\n0.45\n\n26.1%\n38.4%\n46.6%\n54.8%\n\n27.5%\n39.6%\n47.6%\n55.7%\n\nE.2 FLOPs ratios used in the main tables\n\nThe main comparison table uses attention-corrected FLOPs accounting matched to the baseline\nreporting protocol. Let η denote the attention share of the decoder computation. Input-prune methods\nuse\n\nwhile probe-prune methods with a shallow full-context readout proxy use\n\nρ(1 − η(1 − ρ)),\n\nLp + (L − Lp)ρ(1 − η(1 − ρ))\nL\n\n.\n\n(21)\n\n(22)\n\nFor MACER, we set Lp = max(ℓa, ℓv) when reporting estimated FLOPs. This is a conservative\naccounting proxy for the staged implementation because it charges all multimodal tokens until the\nlater video readout rather than crediting the earlier audio-pruning stage. For Qwen2.5-Omni-7B we\nuse L = 28, Lp = 5, and η = 0.235. For Qwen2.5-Omni-3B we use L = 36, Lp = 7, and η = 0.36,\nmatching the scale-specific readout settings in Table 7. Table 18 gives the corresponding MACER\nFLOPs ratios.\n\n25\n\n\fTable 18: Attention-corrected MACER FLOPs ratios computed from Eq. (22). The values are matched\nto the revised main-table FLOPs accounting.\n\nKeep ratio ρ\n\n7B MACER FLOPs ratio\n\n3B MACER FLOPs ratio\n\n0.45\n0.35\n0.25\n\n50.1%\n42.2%\n34.8%\n\n48.5%\n41.0%\n34.1%\n\nE.3 Measured latency table moved to the main paper\n\nMain Table 3 reports the WorldSense latency and memory measurement. We keep the FLOPs\naccounting in this appendix because it defines the architecture-level cost estimate, but the measured\nwall-clock evidence now appears in the main paper so reviewers can inspect it without consulting the\nappendix.\n\nF Cross-Backbone Transfer Study\n\nAdditional backbone: OmniVinci-9B. To test whether MACER’s allocation–selection design is\nspecific to Qwen2.5-Omni, we further evaluate it on OmniVinci-9B [Ye et al., 2025a], an NVLabs\nomnimodal backbone. We keep the shallow readout and one-shot pruning schedule identical to the\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B MACER configuration: audio scores are read at L3, video scores at L5. Thus, the\ntransfer study changes the backbone and sweeps only the retained-audio share α, while keeping the\nvideo coverage strength fixed at λc = 0.20. Table 19 reports the OmniVinci-9B transfer results.\n\nTable 19: Cross-backbone transfer to OmniVinci-9B with explicit capacity split. DailyOmni and\nWorldSense use the corresponding full-token reference runs for normalization. Cons. Norm. Avg.\nnormalizes each benchmark score to the corresponding full-token result and averages across AVUT,\nDailyOmni, and WorldSense. MACER-A (α = 0.20) yields consistent gains across all three retention\nlevels, while MACER-B (α = 0.40) is strongest in the moderate-retention regime (ρ ∈ {35%, 45%}),\nwith mean +7.50 pp over shared top-K. Superscripts mark the full-token reference runs used for\nnormalization.\nMethod\n\nAVUT DailyOmni WorldSense Cons. Norm. Avg. ∆ vs. Shared\n", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 2100, "totalLines": 2604}}}
